{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C68b4sCkyh7mcmA45f5Siq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"MH-DIF on METABENCH MMLU 5,219×12,508 matrix. Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, within-family confound control, domain-specificity, random sanity. Proxy validation against Li et al. web-overlap labels.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n7\t      \"temporal_fpr_li_clean\": 0.318,\n8\t      \"temporal_enrichment_vs_li\": 0.96,\n9\t      \"ability_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n10\t      \"ability_enrichment\": 1.19,\n11\t      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n12\t      \"within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct\": 38.7,\n13\t      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n14\t      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n15\t      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n16\t      \"temporal_ability_jaccard\": 0.014,\n17\t      \"n_ref_temporal\": 1253,\n18\t      \"n_foc_temporal\": 656,\n19\t      \"n_items_tested\": 12508,\n20\t      \"n_ets_c_items\": 3918\n21\t    },\n22\t    \"tables\": [\n23\t      {\n24\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: DIF Detection Summary by Cohort Definition\",\n25\t        \"columns\": [\n26\t          \"cohort\",\n27\t          \"N_ref\",\n28\t          \"N_foc\",\n29\t          \"ETS_A_pct\",\n30\t          \"ETS_B_pct\",\n31\t          \"ETS_C_pct\",\n32\t          \"enrichment_Li\"\n33\t        ],\n34\t        \"rows\": [\n35\t          [\n36\t            \"temporal\",\n37\t            1253,\n38\t            656,\n39\t            52.1,\n40\t            16.6,\n41\t            31.3,\n42\t            0.96\n43\t          ],\n44\t          [\n45\t            \"ability_quintile\",\n46\t            1044,\n47\t            1044,\n48\t            91.8,\n49\t            5.3,\n50\t            2.9,\n51\t            1.19\n52\t          ],\n53\t          [\n54\t            \"random_sanity\",\n55\t            955,\n56\t            955,\n57\t            99.1,\n58\t            0.7,\n59\t            0.18,\n60\t            \"—\"\n61\t          ]\n62\t        ]\n63\t      },\n64\t      {\n65\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: Within-Family Confound Control\",\n66\t        \"columns\": [\n67\t          \"family_pair\",\n68\t          \"N_ref\",\n69\t          \"N_foc\",\n70\t          \"C_pct\",\n71\t          \"overlap_w_temporal_jaccard\"\n72\t        ],\n73\t        \"rows\": [\n74\t          [\n75\t            \"Llama-2 vs Llama-3\",\n76\t            161,\n77\t            308,\n78\t            61.3,\n79\t            0.41\n80\t          ],\n81\t          [\n82\t            \"Mistral vs Mixtral\",\n83\t            89,\n84\t            112,\n85\t            38.7,\n86\t            0.34\n87\t          ]\n88\t        ]\n89\t      }\n90\t    ],\n91\t    \"figures\": [],\n92\t    \"analysis\": \"Temporal DIF at 31.3% is the load-bearing finding for Claim 1. The near-zero random-split sanity check (0.18%) and low ability-cohort C% (2.9%) establish that the temporal signal is real and specific to time, not ability. Within-family results (Llama 61.3%, Mistral 38.7%) show DIF persists even within architecture families, suggesting item instability is not purely a between-family artifact. Enrichment≈0.96 directly motivates the ground truth meta-critique (Claim 2).\\n\\n\\n[DONE — actual registry data] 31.3% C% reflects genuine temporal item instability: model generations 2023→2024-H1 differ in training data composition, RLHF procedures, and data curation practices, all of which change how items function. Enrichment≈0.96 against Li et al. labels is expected and predicted: web-overlap labels capture item existence on the internet (global exposure), not whether an item functions differently across cohorts (differential contamination). DIF detects the latter; the two measures are orthogonal by construction. Random-split sanity (0.18%) confirms the 31.3% is not a statistical artifact of the MH procedure. Domain correlation ρ=0.307 is modest — subjects where models improved more show slightly more DIF, but item-level decomposition (plan_019, done separately) confirms this explains at most 7.6% of the instability.\\n\"\n93\t  },\n94\t  {\n95\t    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n96\t    \"description\": \"Empirically validate DIF's robustness to RL-trained models. Three cohort compositions (SFT-only, +alignment-RL, +reasoning-RL). Traditional detector comparison (Min-K%, ConStat) on 10-15 reasoning-RL models individually.\\n\",\n97\t    \"results\": {\n98\t      \"n_sft_only_models\": 2340,\n99\t      \"n_alignment_rl_models\": 477,\n100\t      \"n_reasoning_rl_models\": 13,\n101\t      \"dif_flag_jaccard_sft_vs_sft_alignrl\": 0.912,\n102\t      \"dif_flag_jaccard_sft_vs_sft_allrl\": 0.874,\n103\t      \"dif_flag_jaccard_alignrl_vs_allrl\": 0.935,\n104\t      \"dif_c_pct_sft_only_cohort\": 31.1,\n105\t      \"dif_c_pct_sft_alignrl_cohort\": 31.5,\n106\t      \"dif_c_pct_sft_allrl_cohort\": 31.8,\n107\t      \"mink_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.563,\n108\t      \"constat_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.614,\n109\t      \"dif_auroc_cohort_with_rl\": 0.831,\n110\t      \"delong_p_dif_vs_mink\": 0.032,\n111\t      \"delong_p_dif_vs_constat\": 0.078,\n112\t      \"tost_equivalence_p\": 0.117,\n113\t      \"bootstrap_auroc_diff_ci_dif_mink\": [\n114\t        0.06,\n115\t        0.33\n116\t      ],\n117\t      \"bootstrap_auroc_diff_ci_dif_constat\": [\n118\t        -0.02,\n119\t        0.28\n120\t      ],\n121\t      \"power_detection_boundary_p\": 0.45,\n122\t      \"power_detection_boundary_delta\": 1.4,\n123\t      \"power_detection_boundary_N\": 80\n124\t    },\n125\t    \"tables\": [\n126\t      {\n127\t        \"name\": \"Table A1: DIF Flag Stability Across Cohort Compositions\",\n128\t        \"columns\": [\n129\t          \"cohort\",\n130\t          \"N_total\",\n131\t          \"C_pct\",\n132\t          \"Jaccard_vs_SFT\"\n133\t        ],\n134\t        \"rows\": [\n135\t          [\n136\t            \"SFT-only\",\n137\t            2340,\n138\t            31.1,\n139\t            1.0\n140\t          ],\n141\t          [\n142\t            \"SFT + alignment-RL\",\n143\t            2817,\n144\t            31.5,\n145\t            0.912\n146\t          ],\n147\t          [\n148\t            \"SFT + all-RL\",\n149\t            2830,\n150\t            31.8,\n151\t            0.874\n152\t          ]\n153\t        ]\n154\t      },\n155\t      {\n156\t        \"name\": \"Table A2: Per-Model Detector Comparison on Reasoning-RL Models\",\n157\t        \"columns\": [\n158\t          \"detector\",\n159\t          \"AUROC\",\n160\t          \"95% CI\",\n161\t          \"degradation_vs_nonRL\"\n162\t        ],\n163\t        \"rows\": [\n164\t          [\n165\t            \"Min-K%\",\n166\t            0.563,\n167\t            \"[0.41, 0.72]\",\n168\t            \"-0.31\"\n169\t          ],\n170\t          [\n171\t            \"ConStat\",\n172\t            0.614,\n173\t            \"[0.46, 0.77]\",\n174\t            \"-0.24\"\n175\t          ],\n176\t          [\n177\t            \"DIF (group)\",\n178\t            0.831,\n179\t            \"[0.74, 0.92]\",\n180\t            \"-0.02\"\n181\t          ]\n182\t        ]\n183\t      }\n184\t    ],\n185\t    \"figures\": [],\n186\t    \"analysis\": \"Results confirm the theoretical argument: DIF's group-level conditioning makes it robust to RL-induced output distribution changes, while per-model detectors (Min-K%, ConStat) degrade substantially. However, N=13 reasoning-RL models precludes formal equivalence testing. Appendix-appropriate: strengthens Claim 1 narrative without being load-bearing. The power surface shows DIF retains detection for δ≥1.4 at p≥0.45, covering the operationally relevant regime (large effects with moderate exploitation).\\n\\n\\nDIF's group-level design conditions on total score, making it fundamentally insensitive to individual model output distributions. Adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a cohort of N≥80 shifts the score distribution marginally (~0.3pp in C%) because MH's quintile-based matching absorbs ability variation. The high Jaccard (0.87-0.91) reflects that DIF flags are driven by the bulk population, not the RL tail. Min-K% degrades to near-chance (AUROC=0.56) because RL training contracts the NLL gap that Min-K% exploits — the per-token likelihood distribution becomes flatter, destroying the memorization signal. ConStat fares slightly better (0.61) because perturbation sensitivity is partially preserved under alignment-RL but substantially reduced under reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO explicitly optimize for robust reasoning). TOST at p=0.117 is inconclusive, honestly reflecting the N=13 limitation. This is presented in limitations as \\\"suggestive evidence of RL robustness, underpowered for formal equivalence.\\\"\\n\"\n187\t  },\n188\t  {\n189\t    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n190\t    \"description\": \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation evaluation on MMLU. Generate paraphrases for 200+ items, compare original vs reformulated performance, apply FDR-controlled detection.\\n\",\n191\t    \"results\": {\n192\t      \"n_items_paraphrased\": 237,\n193\t      \"semantic_similarity_mean\": 0.918,\n194\t      \"human_quality_pct\": 94.1,\n195\t      \"constat_tpr_non_rl_contaminated\": 0.438,\n196\t      \"constat_tpr_rl_contaminated\": 0.172,\n197\t      \"constat_fpr_fdr_q005\": 0.038,\n198\t      \"cohens_d_non_rl\": 0.871,\n199\t      \"cohens_d_rl\": 0.293,\n200\t      \"perf_drop_contaminated_non_rl_pct\": -13.1,\n201\t      \"perf_drop_contaminated_rl_pct\": -4.2,\n202\t      \"perf_drop_clean_pct\": -2.8,\n203\t      \"paraphrase_difficulty_shift_mean\": 0.04,\n204\t      \"n_models_evaluated\": 48\n205\t    },\n206\t    \"tables\": [\n207\t      {\n208\t        \"name\": \"Table A3: ConStat Detection by Model Training Type\",\n209\t        \"columns\": [\n210\t          \"model_type\",\n211\t          \"N_models\",\n212\t          \"TPR\",\n213\t          \"FPR\",\n214\t          \"Cohen_d\",\n215\t          \"perf_drop_pct\"\n216\t        ],\n217\t        \"rows\": [\n218\t          [\n219\t            \"SFT-only\",\n220\t            22,\n221\t            0.438,\n222\t            0.038,\n223\t            0.871,\n224\t            -13.1\n225\t          ],\n226\t          [\n227\t            \"Alignment-RL\",\n228\t            18,\n229\t            0.172,\n230\t            0.041,\n231\t            0.293,\n232\t            -4.2\n233\t          ],\n234\t          [\n235\t            \"Clean (no contamination)\",\n236\t            8,\n237\t            \"—\",\n238\t            0.038,\n239\t            \"—\",\n240\t            -2.8\n241\t          ]\n242\t        ]\n243\t      }\n244\t    ],\n245\t    \"figures\": [],\n246\t    \"analysis\": \"ConStat demonstrates moderate detection of surface memorization in non-RL models but degrades substantially for RL models. This characterizes ConStat's complementary detection niche and motivates ensemble approaches. Appendix-appropriate: validates one component of the ensemble framework (Claim 2 old, now dropped to future work) without being needed for current claims.\\n\\n\\nConStat's detection mechanism exploits surface-level memorization: models that memorized exact item phrasings show large performance drops on semantically equivalent paraphrases. Non-RL models exhibit this cleanly (d=0.87, TPR=0.44) because their memorization is token-sequence-specific. RL-trained models show attenuated signal (d=0.29, TPR=0.17) because reward optimization encourages generalization beyond exact training sequences — the RL objective effectively launders memorization into robust capability, making it indistinguishable from genuine learning. FPR at 0.038 confirms FDR control works. The clean-item performance drop (-2.8%) reflects natural difficulty increase from paraphrasing (slight semantic drift, unfamiliar phrasing) rather than contamination. This complements DIF: ConStat detects per-model surface memorization, DIF detects group-level differential functioning regardless of mechanism.\\n\"\n247\t  },\n248\t  {\n249\t    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n250\t    \"description\": \"DVD generation variance evaluation on MMLU. 50 responses per item via temperature sampling on 10-15 open-weight models. Detect bimodal distributions as contamination signal.\\n\",\n251\t    \"results\": {\n252\t      \"n_models\": 12,\n253\t      \"n_items_evaluated\": 200,\n254\t      \"n_generations_per_item\": 50,\n255\t      \"temperature\": 0.8,\n256\t      \"dvd_tpr_verbatim\": 0.523,\n257\t      \"dvd_tpr_variant\": 0.138,\n258\t      \"dvd_fpr\": 0.062,\n259\t      \"bimodality_coeff_contaminated\": 0.714,\n260\t      \"bimodality_coeff_clean\": 0.283,\n261\t      \"gen_variance_contaminated_mean\": 0.087,\n262\t      \"gen_variance_contaminated_sd\": 0.131,\n263\t      \"gen_variance_clean_mean\": 0.312,\n264\t      \"gen_variance_clean_sd\": 0.108,\n265\t      \"auroc_verbatim\": 0.813,\n266\t      \"auroc_variant\": 0.571,\n267\t      \"gpu_hours_used\": 52\n268\t    },\n269\t    \"tables\": [\n270\t      {\n271\t        \"name\": \"Table A4: DVD Detection by Contamination Type\",\n272\t        \"columns\": [\n273\t          \"contamination_type\",\n274\t          \"N_items\",\n275\t          \"TPR\",\n276\t          \"FPR\",\n277\t          \"bimodality_coeff\",\n278\t          \"mean_gen_variance\"\n279\t        ],\n280\t        \"rows\": [\n281\t          [\n282\t            \"verbatim\",\n283\t            80,\n284\t            0.523,\n285\t            0.062,\n286\t            0.714,\n287\t            0.087\n288\t          ],\n289\t          [\n290\t            \"variant\",\n291\t            60,\n292\t            0.138,\n293\t            0.062,\n294\t            0.391,\n295\t            0.224\n296\t          ],\n297\t          [\n298\t            \"clean\",\n299\t            60,\n300\t            \"—\",\n301\t            0.062,\n302\t            0.283,\n303\t            0.312\n304\t          ]\n305\t        ]\n306\t      }\n307\t    ],\n308\t    \"figures\": [],\n309\t    \"analysis\": \"DVD occupies a narrow but clear detection niche: verbatim exact-match memorization. Its inability to detect variant contamination is a fundamental limitation — generation variance cannot distinguish \\\"learned from contaminated data\\\" from \\\"genuinely competent.\\\" This underscores the complementarity argument for ensemble approaches and the advantage of DIF's mechanism-agnostic group-level detection. Appendix-appropriate: useful for characterizing the detection landscape but not needed for current claims.\\n\\n\\nVerbatim memorization produces near-deterministic outputs — the model has effectively learned a lookup table for memorized items, generating the correct answer with probability >0.95 regardless of temperature. This creates bimodal generation distributions (always correct or fully random) with near-zero variance, easily distinguished from clean items (moderate variance reflecting genuine uncertainty). Variant contamination does NOT produce this bimodality because the model learned the underlying concept rather than the exact answer string — generation variance remains moderate, and DVD cannot distinguish \\\"learned from training data\\\" from \\\"genuinely knows.\\\" This limits DVD to a specific niche: detecting exact-match verbatim memorization of item text + answer. Complementary to DIF (group-level, mechanism-agnostic) and ConStat (perturbation-based, per-model).\\n\"\n310\t  },\n311\t  {\n312\t    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n313\t    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Majority voting and weighted fusion. Coverage analysis by contamination mechanism.\\n\",\n314\t    \"results\": {\n315\t      \"dif_alone_tpr\": 0.682,\n316\t      \"constat_alone_tpr\": 0.438,\n317\t      \"dvd_alone_tpr\": 0.523,\n318\t      \"majority_2of3_tpr\": 0.731,\n319\t      \"majority_2of3_fpr\": 0.028,\n320\t      \"majority_2of3_precision\": 0.921,\n321\t      \"weighted_fusion_tpr\": 0.778,\n322\t      \"weighted_fusion_fpr\": 0.057,\n323\t      \"weighted_fusion_precision\": 0.883,\n324\t      \"uniform_weight_tpr\": 0.752,\n325\t      \"uniform_weight_fpr\": 0.044,\n326\t      \"optimal_weights_dif\": 0.43,\n327\t      \"optimal_weights_constat\": 0.32,\n328\t      \"optimal_weights_dvd\": 0.25,\n329\t      \"cv_weight_stability_cv\": 0.13,\n330\t      \"coverage_dif_only_pct\": 22.8,\n331\t      \"coverage_constat_only_pct\": 13.6,\n332\t      \"coverage_dvd_only_pct\": 8.1,\n333\t      \"coverage_multi_signal_pct\": 55.5\n334\t    },\n335\t    \"tables\": [\n336\t      {\n337\t        \"name\": \"Table A5: Ensemble Detection Performance\",\n338\t        \"columns\": [\n339\t          \"strategy\",\n340\t          \"TPR\",\n341\t          \"FPR\",\n342\t          \"precision\",\n343\t          \"F1\"\n344\t        ],\n345\t        \"rows\": [\n346\t          [\n347\t            \"DIF only\",\n348\t            0.682,\n349\t            0.036,\n350\t            0.892,\n351\t            0.773\n352\t          ],\n353\t          [\n354\t            \"ConStat only\",\n355\t            0.438,\n356\t            0.038,\n357\t            0.854,\n358\t            0.579\n359\t          ],\n360\t          [\n361\t            \"DVD only\",\n362\t            0.523,\n363\t            0.062,\n364\t            0.809,\n365\t            0.635\n366\t          ],\n367\t          [\n368\t            \"2-of-3 majority\",\n369\t            0.731,\n370\t            0.028,\n371\t            0.921,\n372\t            0.815\n373\t          ],\n374\t          [\n375\t            \"Weighted fusion\",\n376\t            0.778,\n377\t            0.057,\n378\t            0.883,\n379\t            0.827\n380\t          ],\n381\t          [\n382\t            \"Uniform weights\",\n383\t            0.752,\n384\t            0.044,\n385\t            0.893,\n386\t            0.817\n387\t          ]\n388\t        ]\n389\t      }\n390\t    ],\n391\t    \"figures\": [],\n392\t    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble improves coverage but is not needed for current paper claims (Claim 2 pivoted from ensemble to ground truth meta-critique). Results support future work direction: multi-signal contamination detection as a practical pipeline. The 22.8% DIF-only coverage confirms DIF detects contamination modes invisible to per-model methods. Appendix-appropriate: demonstrates feasibility without being load-bearing.\\n\\n\\nThe three detectors exploit orthogonal contamination dimensions: DIF measures differential group-level item functioning (mechanism-agnostic, requires cohort comparison), ConStat measures per-model perturbation sensitivity (targets surface memorization), DVD measures generation variance (targets verbatim memorization). Their error patterns are weakly correlated because they target different aspects of contamination. Majority voting suppresses false positives (FPR=0.028) by requiring convergent evidence from 2+ independent mechanisms. Weighted fusion gains ~5pp TPR over majority voting at the cost of higher FPR (0.057) by allowing strong single-signal evidence to override absent signals. Optimal weights favor DIF (0.43) because it has the broadest coverage (mechanism-agnostic, group-level), followed by ConStat (0.32) and DVD (0.25). The modest improvement over uniform weights reflects limited calibration data.\\n\"\n393\t  },\n394\t  {\n395\t    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n396\t    \"description\": \"GSM8K DIF replication (1,319 items × 6,702 models). Semi-synthetic validation. GSM1K contamination gap correlation (N=33).\\n\",\n397\t    \"results\": {\n398\t      \"step1_n_items\": 1319,\n399\t      \"step1_n_ref\": 1017,\n400\t      \"step1_n_foc\": 804,\n401\t      \"step1_standard_c_pct\": 18.9,\n402\t      \"step1_external_c_pct\": 47.4,\n403\t      \"step1_sanity_c_pct\": 0.3,\n404\t      \"step1_standard_external_spearman\": 0.926,\n405\t      \"step2_standard_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n406\t      \"step2_external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.517,\n407\t      \"step3_n_models\": 33,\n408\t      \"step3_pearson_r\": -0.478,\n409\t      \"step3_pearson_p\": 0.005,\n410\t      \"step3_spearman_rho\": -0.403,\n411\t      \"step3_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n412\t      \"step3_partial_r\": -0.385,\n413\t      \"step3_partial_p\": 0.027,\n414\t      \"step3_bca_ci_pearson\": [\n415\t        -0.714,\n416\t        -0.155\n417\t      ],\n418\t      \"step3_bca_ci_spearman\": [\n419\t        -0.687,\n420\t        -0.031\n421\t      ],\n422\t      \"step3_bca_ci_partial\": [\n423\t        -0.64,\n424\t        -0.058\n425\t      ],\n426\t      \"step3_permutation_p\": 0.014,\n427\t      \"step3_cook_d_max_model\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n428\t      \"step3_leave_one_out_r_range\": [\n429\t        -0.537,\n430\t        -0.374\n431\t      ]\n432\t    },\n433\t    \"tables\": [\n434\t      {\n435\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: GSM8K DIF Summary\",\n436\t        \"columns\": [\n437\t          \"matching\",\n438\t          \"C_pct\",\n439\t          \"sanity_pct\",\n440\t          \"ΔTPR_p10\"\n441\t        ],\n442\t        \"rows\": [\n443\t          [\n444\t            \"standard (total score)\",\n445\t            18.9,\n446\t            0.3,\n447\t            0.818\n448\t          ],\n449\t          [\n450\t            \"external (cross-benchmark)\",\n451\t            47.4,\n452\t            \"—\",\n453\t            0.517\n454\t          ]\n455\t        ]\n456\t      },\n457\t      {\n458\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: GSM1K Contamination Gap Correlation\",\n459\t        \"columns\": [\n460\t          \"statistic\",\n461\t          \"value\",\n462\t          \"p\",\n463\t          \"95% BCa CI\"\n464\t        ],\n465\t        \"rows\": [\n466\t          [\n467\t            \"Pearson r\",\n468\t            -0.478,\n469\t            0.005,\n470\t            \"[-0.714, -0.155]\"\n471\t          ],\n472\t          [\n473\t            \"Spearman ρ\",\n474\t            -0.403,\n475\t            0.02,\n476\t            \"[-0.687, -0.031]\"\n477\t          ],\n478\t          [\n479\t            \"Partial r\",\n480\t            -0.385,\n481\t            0.027,\n482\t            \"[-0.640, -0.058]\"\n483\t          ]\n484\t        ]\n485\t      }\n486\t    ],\n487\t    \"figures\": [],\n488\t    \"analysis\": \"GSM8K replication confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (Claim 3). GSM1K anti-correlation provides the fourth layer of Claim 4 (downstream impact). Under best tier, reviewers treat r=−0.478 as suggestive-but-consistent rather than attacking it as underpowered — the convergent evidence (all CIs exclude zero, permutation p=0.014, leave-one-out stable) is sufficient for a discovery paper. Plan_017 redundancy result (ρ≈0.997) independently supports Claim 4 even if Step 3 is discounted.\\n\\n\\n[DONE — actual registry data] GSM8K temporal DIF at 18.9% (vs MMLU 31.3%) reflects GSM8K's more homogeneous construct (single-domain math vs 57-subject MMLU) — fewer dimensions of variation means fewer items whose functioning changes across generations. Semi-synthetic ΔTPR=+0.818 confirms the detection framework ports to single-domain benchmarks with high power. Step 3 anti-correlation (r=−0.478) is the key downstream finding: models with larger GSM8K-GSM1K contamination gaps show disproportionately LOWER performance on DIF-C items. This is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items (memorized items that function uniformly across cohorts), while DIF items capture genuine capability differences that contamination cannot inflate. All BCa CIs exclude zero, and leave-one-out analysis confirms robustness to individual influential models. N=33 is honestly acknowledged as suggestive — the finding is consistent but a larger matched sample would strengthen it.\\n\"\n489\t  },\n490\t  {\n491\t    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n492\t    \"description\": \"Bifactor IRT model on METABENCH MMLU (general + 57 subject factors). Anchor item selection. Fixed-parameter equating pilot.\\n\",\n493\t    \"results\": {\n494\t      \"general_factor_variance_pct\": 58.3,\n495\t      \"subject_specific_variance_mean_pct\": 30.8,\n496\t      \"residual_variance_pct\": 10.9,\n497\t      \"bifactor_rmsea\": 0.019,\n498\t      \"bifactor_cfi\": 0.953,\n499\t      \"bifactor_tli\": 0.941,\n500\t      \"unidim_rmsea\": 0.035,\n501\t      \"unidim_cfi\": 0.882,\n502\t      \"unidim_tli\": 0.864,\n503\t      \"n_anchor_items\": 5640,\n504\t      \"anchor_criteria\": \"no DIF, rpbis>0.20, general loading>0.30\",\n505\t      \"within_subject_dif_after_bifactor_pct\": 35.8,\n506\t      \"within_subject_dif_unidim_pct\": 38.1,\n507\t      \"dif_reduction_from_bifactor_pp\": 2.3,\n508\t      \"equating_rho_general\": 0.961,\n509\t      \"equating_rho_subject_range\": [\n510\t        0.891,\n511\t        0.974\n512\t      ],\n513\t      \"equating_rho_subject_below_095\": 8,\n514\t      \"convergence_em_iterations\": 2140,\n515\t      \"convergence_achieved\": true\n516\t    },\n517\t    \"tables\": [\n518\t      {\n519\t        \"name\": \"Table A6: Model Fit Comparison\",\n520\t        \"columns\": [\n521\t          \"model\",\n522\t          \"RMSEA\",\n523\t          \"CFI\",\n524\t          \"TLI\",\n525\t          \"n_params\"\n526\t        ],\n527\t        \"rows\": [\n528\t          [\n529\t            \"Unidimensional\",\n530\t            0.035,\n531\t            0.882,\n532\t            0.864,\n533\t            25016\n534\t          ],\n535\t          [\n536\t            \"Bifactor (1+57)\",\n537\t            0.019,\n538\t            0.953,\n539\t            0.941,\n540\t            37524\n541\t          ]\n542\t        ]\n543\t      },\n544\t      {\n545\t        \"name\": \"Table A7: DIF Persistence After Bifactor Conditioning\",\n546\t        \"columns\": [\n547\t          \"conditioning\",\n548\t          \"within_subject_C_pct\",\n549\t          \"reduction_pp\"\n550\t        ],\n551\t        \"rows\": [\n552\t          [\n553\t            \"None (unidimensional)\",\n554\t            38.1,\n555\t            \"—\"\n556\t          ],\n557\t          [\n558\t            \"Bifactor general+specific\",\n559\t            35.8,\n560\t            2.3\n561\t          ]\n562\t        ]\n563\t      }\n564\t    ],\n565\t    \"figures\": [],\n566\t    \"analysis\": \"Bifactor's 2.3pp DIF reduction (~6% relative) confirms that >90% of temporal DIF is genuine item-level instability, not dimensional misfit artifact. This is the strongest possible evidence for Claim 1's robustness to IRT model specification concerns. The equating pilot demonstrates feasibility but remains below the quality bar for a main-body claim — consistent with paper_framing's decision to relegate this to appendix. The fit improvement (RMSEA halved) is itself valuable: it confirms the diagnosis that 98% |Lz|>2 reflects multidimensionality, not fundamental model invalidity.\\n\\n\\nBifactor correctly identifies MMLU's multidimensional structure — 57 subjects create substantial specific factors (31% of variance). However, DIF persists at 35.8% even after extracting subject-specific variance (only 2.3pp reduction from 38.1%), confirming that the instability is genuinely item-level, not an artifact of dimensional confounding. The general factor dominates (58%) because MMLU subjects share a common \\\"general LLM ability\\\" axis — models good at one subject tend to be good at others. IRT fit improves substantially (RMSEA 0.035→0.019, CFI 0.88→0.95), validating that multidimensionality was the primary source of unidimensional misfit, but this improved fit does not resolve DIF. Equating at ρ=0.961 on the general factor demonstrates feasibility of anchor-based score maintenance, but 8/57 subjects fall below ρ=0.95 (small-N subjects with <50 items where anchor selection is constrained). This supports future work on benchmark maintenance but is appropriately appendix material — the finding that bifactor doesn't resolve DIF is itself evidence for Claim 1's robustness.\\n\"\n567\t  },\n568\t  {\n569\t    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n570\t    \"description\": \"LLM-based replacement item generation for DIF-flagged MMLU items. Prompt-based vs student-simulation difficulty estimation. Holdout validation.\\n\",\n571\t    \"results\": {\n572\t      \"n_items_generated\": 500,\n573\t      \"n_target_items\": 250,\n574\t      \"candidates_per_target\": 2,\n575\t      \"difficulty_rho_prompt\": 0.362,\n576\t      \"difficulty_rho_simulation\": 0.731,\n577\t      \"difficulty_rho_best_candidate\": 0.793,\n578\t      \"construct_alignment_expert_pct\": 88.7,\n579\t      \"discrimination_rpbis_rho\": 0.514,\n580\t      \"holdout_within_015_difficulty_pct\": 83.6,\n581\t      \"distractor_quality_plausible_pct\": 76.2,\n582\t      \"simulation_panel_n_models\": 30,\n583\t      \"gpu_hours_used\": 164\n584\t    },\n585\t    \"tables\": [\n586\t      {\n587\t        \"name\": \"Table A8: Difficulty Estimation Comparison\",\n588\t        \"columns\": [\n589\t          \"method\",\n590\t          \"ρ_with_target\",\n591\t          \"MAE_difficulty\",\n592\t          \"coverage\"\n593\t        ],\n594\t        \"rows\": [\n595\t          [\n596\t            \"Prompt-based targeting\",\n597\t            0.362,\n598\t            0.183,\n599\t            \"100%\"\n600\t          ],\n601\t          [\n602\t            \"Student simulation (30 models)\",\n603\t            0.731,\n604\t            0.094,\n605\t            \"100%\"\n606\t          ],\n607\t          [\n608\t            \"Best of 2 candidates\",\n609\t            0.793,\n610\t            0.078,\n611\t            \"100%\"\n612\t          ]\n613\t        ]\n614\t      }\n615\t    ],\n616\t    \"figures\": [],\n617\t    \"analysis\": \"The prompt→simulation gap (ρ=0.36→0.73) is the key practical finding: empirical calibration is essential for item replacement pipelines. Appendix-appropriate: supports future benchmark regeneration work but is below the quality bar for a main-body claim (consistent with paper_framing's decision to drop regeneration from claims). The 76% distractor quality suggests human review remains necessary for production deployment.\\n\\n\\nLLMs generate on-topic items reliably (89% construct alignment) because they understand MMLU subject boundaries and question formats. However, prompt-based difficulty targeting fails (ρ=0.36) because the generator lacks fine-grained knowledge of what makes an item hard for OTHER LLMs — difficulty is an emergent property of item-population interaction, not an intrinsic text feature. Student simulation (using 30 diverse models as examinees) provides empirical difficulty estimates that capture actual model behavior, achieving ρ=0.73 — approaching the r=0.75-0.82 reported in the student simulation literature (arXiv 2601.09953). Best-candidate selection from 2 options improves to ρ=0.79 by choosing the candidate closer to target difficulty. Distractor quality (76%) is the weakest link: generating plausible wrong answers for multiple-choice items requires understanding common misconceptions, which LLMs model imperfectly. This finding supports the conclusion that automated item generation is feasible but requires empirical calibration — prompt-based difficulty targeting alone is insufficient.\\n\"\n618\t  },\n619\t  {\n620\t    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n621\t    \"description\": \"Bayesian cumulative DIF priors across 4 simulated benchmark administrations. Power accumulation for medium effects (δ=1.0).\\n\",\n622\t    \"results\": {\n623\t      \"n_administrations\": 4,\n624\t      \"n_per_admin\": 80,\n625\t      \"delta_simulated\": 1.0,\n626\t      \"individual_power_mean\": 0.415,\n627\t      \"individual_power_range\": [\n628\t        0.398,\n629\t        0.432\n630\t      ],\n631\t      \"cumulative_power_after_2\": 0.576,\n632\t      \"cumulative_power_after_3\": 0.718,\n633\t      \"cumulative_power_after_4\": 0.812,\n634\t      \"fpr_across_accumulations_max\": 0.048,\n635\t      \"bf10_after_1\": 2.14,\n636\t      \"bf10_after_2\": 4.83,\n637\t      \"bf10_after_3\": 9.27,\n638\t      \"bf10_after_4\": 16.7,\n639\t      \"bf10_moderate_evidence_threshold\": 3.0,\n640\t      \"power_at_bf10_gt3_after_2\": 0.648,\n641\t      \"mh_individual_power_at_delta10\": 0.383,\n642\t      \"power_gain_cumulative_vs_individual\": \"+0.303 (after 3)\"\n643\t    },\n644\t    \"tables\": [\n645\t      {\n646\t        \"name\": \"Table A9: Cumulative Bayesian Power by Administration\",\n647\t        \"columns\": [\n648\t          \"n_admins\",\n649\t          \"cumulative_N\",\n650\t          \"power\",\n651\t          \"BF10\",\n652\t          \"FPR\"\n653\t        ],\n654\t        \"rows\": [\n655\t          [\n656\t            1,\n657\t            80,\n658\t            0.415,\n659\t            2.14,\n660\t            0.042\n661\t          ],\n662\t          [\n663\t            2,\n664\t            160,\n665\t            0.576,\n666\t            4.83,\n667\t            0.044\n668\t          ],\n669\t          [\n670\t            3,\n671\t            240,\n672\t            0.718,\n673\t            9.27,\n674\t            0.046\n675\t          ],\n676\t          [\n677\t            4,\n678\t            320,\n679\t            0.812,\n680\t            16.7,\n681\t            0.048\n682\t          ]\n683\t        ]\n684\t      }\n685\t    ],\n686\t    \"figures\": [],\n687\t    \"analysis\": \"Simulation-only result (no real data). Confirms theoretical prediction that Bayesian cumulative DIF closes the power gap for medium effects. The practical implication: benchmark monitoring programs can detect δ=1.0 DIF after 3 waves of ~80 models each, even though no single wave has adequate power. Appendix-appropriate: methodological contribution for future longitudinal monitoring, not needed for current claims. Underdeveloped for main-body inclusion — needs real multi-wave data to validate.\\n\\n\\nIndividual MH tests at N=80, δ=1.0 have insufficient power (~0.42) — this is the \\\"power gap\\\" identified in phase 2 simulations (pass criterion required δ≥1.4). Bayesian updating converts individually inconclusive tests into cumulative evidence: the posterior from each administration constrains the prior for the next. After 3 administrations, cumulative power crosses 0.70 (the conventional adequate-power threshold) because the accumulated evidence from 3×80=240 effective observations exceeds what any single N=80 test provides. The Bayes factor trajectory (2.1→4.8→9.3→16.7) follows the expected log-linear accumulation under consistent DIF. FPR remains controlled (≤0.048) because the Bayesian framework naturally penalizes inconsistent evidence — items with spurious DIF in one administration but not others see their posterior regress toward the null. This addresses the practical concern that benchmark monitoring requires repeated measurement, not one-shot detection.\\n\"\n688\t  },\n689\t  {\n690\t    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n691\t    \"description\": \"Progressive item replacement validity analysis. Ranking stability, score reliability, construct coverage as function of replacement rate (5-50%).\\n\",\n692\t    \"results\": {\n693\t      \"replacement_rates\": [\n694\t        0.05,\n695\t        0.1,\n696\t        0.2,\n697\t        0.31,\n698\t        0.5\n699\t      ],\n700\t      \"rho_at_005\": 0.9993,\n701\t      \"rho_at_010\": 0.9984,\n702\t      \"rho_at_020\": 0.9968,\n703\t      \"rho_at_031\": 0.9951,\n704\t      \"rho_at_050\": 0.9912,\n705\t      \"alpha_full\": 0.9937,\n706\t      \"alpha_at_031\": 0.9882,\n707\t      \"alpha_at_050\": 0.9791,\n708\t      \"construct_coverage_at_031\": \"57/57\",\n709\t      \"construct_coverage_at_050\": \"56/57\",\n710\t      \"construct_coverage_threshold_subjects_below_5items\": 0.62,\n711\t      \"validity_threshold_rho_lt_099\": 0.453,\n712\t      \"shr_full\": 0.9934,\n713\t      \"shr_at_031\": 0.9881,\n714\t      \"shr_at_050\": 0.9763,\n715\t      \"replacement_strategy\": \"DIF-flagged items replaced by matched-difficulty anchors\"\n716\t    },\n717\t    \"tables\": [\n718\t      {\n719\t        \"name\": \"Table A10: Validity Degradation Under Progressive Replacement\",\n720\t        \"columns\": [\n721\t          \"replacement_pct\",\n722\t          \"ρ_ranking\",\n723\t          \"α_reliability\",\n724\t          \"SHR\",\n725\t          \"construct_coverage\"\n726\t        ],\n727\t        \"rows\": [\n728\t          [\n729\t            0,\n730\t            1.0,\n731\t            0.994,\n732\t            0.993,\n733\t            \"57/57\"\n734\t          ],\n735\t          [\n736\t            5,\n737\t            0.999,\n738\t            0.993,\n739\t            0.993,\n740\t            \"57/57\"\n741\t          ],\n742\t          [\n743\t            10,\n744\t            0.998,\n745\t            0.992,\n746\t            0.992,\n747\t            \"57/57\"\n748\t          ],\n749\t          [\n750\t            20,\n751\t            0.997,\n752\t            0.99,\n753\t            0.99,\n754\t            \"57/57\"\n755\t          ],\n756\t          [\n757\t            31,\n758\t            0.995,\n759\t            0.988,\n760\t            0.988,\n761\t            \"57/57\"\n762\t          ],\n763\t          [\n764\t            50,\n765\t            0.991,\n766\t            0.979,\n767\t            0.976,\n768\t            \"56/57\"\n769\t          ]\n770\t        ]\n771\t      }\n772\t    ],\n773\t    \"figures\": [],\n774\t    \"analysis\": \"Directly supports the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing: replacing 31% of items (all DIF-flagged) preserves >99.5% of ranking information, demonstrating massive measurement redundancy. The convex degradation curve means benchmark maintainers have large headroom for item updates. Appendix-appropriate: clean supplementary result that reinforces Claim 4 (redundancy layer) without adding new claims.\\n\\n\\nMMLU's massive redundancy (12,508 items, most subjects with 100+ items) provides enormous buffering against item replacement. Ranking stability at 31% replacement (ρ=0.9951) closely matches plan_017's DIF removal result (ρ=0.997) — the slight discrepancy reflects calibration noise in replacement items vs clean removal. The curve is convex: initial replacement costs almost nothing (ρ=0.9993 at 5%) because replaced items are drawn from the unstable pool (high DIF, contributing noise rather than signal). The validity threshold (ρ<0.99) is not crossed until ~45% replacement, demonstrating that even aggressive maintenance preserves benchmark validity. Construct coverage holds at 57/57 subjects through 31% because DIF items are distributed across subjects (not clustered in a few), so no subject loses all its items. Score reliability drops only 0.55pp (0.994→0.988) at 31% because Cronbach's α is driven by total item count and inter-item correlation, both of which remain high after replacement.\\n\"\n775\t  },\n776\t  {\n777\t    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n778\t    \"description\": \"Item-Focused Trees with continuous release date covariate. Compare detection power and flexibility vs categorical MH-DIF.\\n\",\n779\t    \"results\": {\n780\t      \"n_models_with_release_date\": 3412,\n781\t      \"ift_significant_items_pct\": 33.8,\n782\t      \"ift_mh_overlap_jaccard\": 0.762,\n783\t      \"ift_only_items_pct\": 8.3,\n784\t      \"mh_only_items_pct\": 5.2,\n785\t      \"natural_breakpoints\": [\n786\t        \"2023-03-15\",\n787\t        \"2023-08-22\",\n788\t        \"2024-01-10\"\n789\t      ],\n790\t      \"n_terminal_nodes\": 4,\n791\t      \"release_date_variable_importance\": 0.71,\n792\t      \"param_count_importance\": 0.14,\n793\t      \"training_data_size_importance\": 0.09,\n794\t      \"mean_dif_early_2023\": 0.82,\n795\t      \"mean_dif_late_2023\": 1.21,\n796\t      \"mean_dif_early_2024\": 1.48,\n797\t      \"dif_magnitude_release_date_r\": 0.391,\n798\t      \"gradual_drift_items_pct\": 8.3,\n799\t      \"sharp_transition_items_pct\": 5.2\n800\t    },\n801\t    \"tables\": [\n802\t      {\n803\t        \"name\": \"Table A11: IFT vs MH-DIF Detection Comparison\",\n804\t        \"columns\": [\n805\t          \"method\",\n806\t          \"significant_items_pct\",\n807\t          \"unique_items_pct\",\n808\t          \"natural_breakpoints\"\n809\t        ],\n810\t        \"rows\": [\n811\t          [\n812\t            \"MH categorical\",\n813\t            31.3,\n814\t            5.2,\n815\t            \"2023 vs 2024-H1 (manual)\"\n816\t          ],\n817\t          [\n818\t            \"IFT continuous\",\n819\t            33.8,\n820\t            8.3,\n821\t            \"Mar-23, Aug-23, Jan-24 (data-driven)\"\n822\t          ]\n823\t        ]\n824\t      },\n825\t      {\n826\t        \"name\": \"Table A12: IFT Variable Importance\",\n827\t        \"columns\": [\n828\t          \"covariate\",\n829\t          \"importance\",\n830\t          \"interpretation\"\n831\t        ],\n832\t        \"rows\": [\n833\t          [\n834\t            \"release_date\",\n835\t            0.71,\n836\t            \"temporal position drives DIF\"\n837\t          ],\n838\t          [\n839\t            \"param_count\",\n840\t            0.14,\n841\t            \"larger models show slightly different DIF\"\n842\t          ],\n843\t          [\n844\t            \"training_data_size\",\n845\t            0.09,\n846\t            \"marginal contribution\"\n847\t          ]\n848\t        ]\n849\t      }\n850\t    ],\n851\t    \"figures\": [],\n852\t    \"analysis\": \"IFT provides a complementary lens: confirms the temporal DIF finding is robust to the choice of categorical split point, and reveals gradual drift items missed by binary comparison. The natural breakpoints are empirically interesting (potential link to model release waves) but not developed into a claim. Appendix-appropriate: robustness check that strengthens Claim 1 without adding complexity to the main narrative.\\n\\n\\nIFT treats release date as a continuous covariate, revealing temporal DIF structure that categorical splits can miss. The 76% Jaccard overlap with MH confirms that the binary 2023/2024-H1 split captures most of the signal — the categorical approximation is adequate for the primary analysis. The additional 8.3% IFT-only items are \\\"gradual drifters\\\" whose functioning changes smoothly across the timeline without a single dramatic shift — these are invisible to any binary split but accumulate meaningful DIF over the full period. Natural breakpoints (March 2023, August 2023, January 2024) likely correspond to major model release waves (Llama-2 era, post-ChatGPT proliferation, GPT-4 derivatives). The monotonic DIF magnitude increase (0.82→1.21→1.48) confirms genuine temporal trend rather than categorical artifact. Release date dominates variable importance (0.71) over model size (0.14) — WHEN matters more than HOW BIG, consistent with training data composition / contamination explaining instability rather than raw capability scaling.\\n\"\n853\t  },\n854\t  {\n855\t    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n856\t    \"description\": \"DIF within decoder-only-only subpopulation. Test whether architecture heterogeneity removal improves IRT fit and reduces spurious DIF.\\n\",\n857\t    \"results\": {\n858\t      \"total_models\": 5227,\n859\t      \"decoder_only_models\": 5226,\n860\t      \"decoder_only_pct\": 99.98,\n861\t      \"non_decoder_models\": 1,\n862\t      \"c_pct_full\": 31.3,\n863\t      \"c_pct_decoder_only\": 31.3,\n864\t      \"jaccard_full_vs_decoder\": 1.0,\n865\t      \"irt_rmsea_decoder_only\": 0.033,\n866\t      \"irt_rmsea_full\": 0.034,\n867\t      \"lz_gt2_pct_decoder_only\": 97.9,\n868\t      \"lz_gt2_pct_full\": 98.0,\n869\t      \"delta_mh_correlation_full_vs_decoder\": 0.9999\n870\t    },\n871\t    \"tables\": [\n872\t      {\n873\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Architecture Stratification\",\n874\t        \"columns\": [\n875\t          \"population\",\n876\t          \"N\",\n877\t          \"C_pct\",\n878\t          \"RMSEA\",\n879\t          \"|Lz|>2 pct\"\n880\t        ],\n881\t        \"rows\": [\n882\t          [\n883\t            \"Full\",\n884\t            5227,\n885\t            31.3,\n886\t            0.034,\n887\t            \"98.0%\"\n888\t          ],\n889\t          [\n890\t            \"Decoder-only\",\n891\t            5226,\n892\t            31.3,\n893\t            0.033,\n894\t            \"97.9%\"\n895\t          ]\n896\t        ]\n897\t      }\n898\t    ],\n899\t    \"figures\": [],\n900\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the most decisive robustness check in the paper: the architecture confound concern is dissolved empirically rather than argued away theoretically. Main-body appropriate as part of Claim 1 robustness (§4). The finding that the LLM ecosystem is architecturally homogeneous has broader implications for psychometric analyses of LLM benchmarks — the \\\"examinee population\\\" is far more homogeneous than human testing populations, which simplifies DIF analysis but raises questions about construct validity.\\n\\n\\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227), reflecting the empirical reality that virtually all LLMs evaluated on MMLU since 2023 are autoregressive transformers. The single non-decoder model has zero statistical influence on any group-level analysis — removing it produces Jaccard=1.0 because MH chi-squared tests are robust to single-observation perturbations in large samples. IRT fit does not improve (RMSEA 0.034→0.033) because the misfit (98% |Lz|>2) is driven by MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity. This result definitively dissolves the worst-case reviewer concern about architecture confounding. The architecture homogeneity is itself an empirical finding: the LLM evaluation ecosystem has converged on decoder-only transformers, making architecture-based stratification unnecessary for this population. Future benchmarks with more diverse architectures (e.g., state-space models, hybrid architectures) would need to revisit this analysis.\\n\"\n901\t  },\n902\t  {\n903\t    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n904\t    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into real METABENCH response matrix. Measure DIF detection power under controlled ground truth. Vary injection parameters.\\n\",\n905\t    \"results\": {\n906\t      \"temporal_external_tpr_p0\": 0.326,\n907\t      \"temporal_external_tpr_p03\": 0.548,\n908\t      \"temporal_external_tpr_p05\": 0.834,\n909\t      \"temporal_external_tpr_p07\": 0.99,\n910\t      \"temporal_external_tpr_p10\": 1.0,\n911\t      \"temporal_external_fpr\": 0.314,\n912\t      \"temporal_external_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n913\t      \"temporal_external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n914\t      \"cross_subject_delta_fpr\": 0.0,\n915\t      \"within_subject_fpr_inflation_p10\": 0.186,\n916\t      \"ability_external_tpr_p10\": 0.8,\n917\t      \"ability_external_fpr\": 0.493,\n918\t      \"dose_response_monotonic\": true,\n919\t      \"matching_variable_contamination_confirmed\": true\n920\t    },\n921\t    \"tables\": [\n922\t      {\n923\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Semi-Synthetic Detection Power (Cross-Subject Matching)\",\n924\t        \"columns\": [\n925\t          \"exploitation_p\",\n926\t          \"TPR\",\n927\t          \"ΔTPR\",\n928\t          \"FPR\",\n929\t          \"ΔFPR\"\n930\t        ],\n931\t        \"rows\": [\n932\t          [\n933\t            0.0,\n934\t            0.326,\n935\t            0.0,\n936\t            0.314,\n937\t            0.0\n938\t          ],\n939\t          [\n940\t            0.3,\n941\t            0.548,\n942\t            0.222,\n943\t            0.314,\n944\t            0.0\n945\t          ],\n946\t          [\n947\t            0.5,\n948\t            0.834,\n949\t            0.508,\n950\t            0.314,\n951\t            0.0\n952\t          ],\n953\t          [\n954\t            0.7,\n955\t            0.99,\n956\t            0.664,\n957\t            0.314,\n958\t            0.0\n959\t          ],\n960\t          [\n961\t            1.0,\n962\t            1.0,\n963\t            0.674,\n964\t            0.314,\n965\t            0.0\n966\t          ]\n967\t        ]\n968\t      },\n969\t      {\n970\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Matching Variable Comparison\",\n971\t        \"columns\": [\n972\t          \"matching\",\n973\t          \"ΔFPR_p10\",\n974\t          \"ΔTPR_p05\",\n975\t          \"FPR_inflation\"\n976\t        ],\n977\t        \"rows\": [\n978\t          [\n979\t            \"Within-subject\",\n980\t            0.186,\n981\t            \"varies\",\n982\t            \"YES\"\n983\t          ],\n984\t          [\n985\t            \"Cross-subject\",\n986\t            0.0,\n987\t            0.508,\n988\t            \"NO\"\n989\t          ]\n990\t        ]\n991\t      }\n992\t    ],\n993\t    \"figures\": [],\n994\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the primary validation for Claim 3 (semi-synthetic framework) and provides controlled ground truth addressing the circularity concern in Claim 2. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is the key methodological contribution — it solves the matching variable contamination problem that plagues within-subject DIF when the conditioning variable is endogenous. Main-body material for §4.\\n\\n[DONE — actual registry data] Semi-synthetic injection into real response matrices provides the controlled ground truth that proxy labels cannot. ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 demonstrates that DIF detects differential contamination when the exploitation rate is moderate — this is the core validation for the detection claim. The dose-response curve (p=0.3→0.5→0.7→1.0 maps to ΔTPR=0.22→0.51→0.66→0.67) confirms detection power scales with contamination intensity, as expected from the DIF effect size formula (Δ_MH ∝ log odds ratio of correct response, which increases with exploitation probability). Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000) because the matching variable (total score on OTHER subjects) is causally independent of contamination injected into a specific subject. Within-subject matching shows FPR inflation (+0.186) because contaminating items in a subject inflates total score on that subject, which contaminates the matching variable. This distinction is a subtle but general methodological insight: any DIF application where the matching variable can be affected by the grouping variable will show FPR inflation.\\n\"\n995\t  }\n996\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78c9e633-a2af-4fcb-9127-d0b2b92ccab0","uuid":"b6e89f4d-a032-4779-a3ce-10df6e6980ab","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:54:35.537Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n    \"description\": \"MH-DIF on METABENCH MMLU 5,219×12,508 matrix. Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, within-family confound control, domain-specificity, random sanity. Proxy validation against Li et al. web-overlap labels.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n      \"temporal_fpr_li_clean\": 0.318,\n      \"temporal_enrichment_vs_li\": 0.96,\n      \"ability_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n      \"ability_enrichment\": 1.19,\n      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n      \"within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct\": 38.7,\n      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n      \"temporal_ability_jaccard\": 0.014,\n      \"n_ref_temporal\": 1253,\n      \"n_foc_temporal\": 656,\n      \"n_items_tested\": 12508,\n      \"n_ets_c_items\": 3918\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: DIF Detection Summary by Cohort Definition\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"cohort\",\n          \"N_ref\",\n          \"N_foc\",\n          \"ETS_A_pct\",\n          \"ETS_B_pct\",\n          \"ETS_C_pct\",\n          \"enrichment_Li\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"temporal\",\n            1253,\n            656,\n            52.1,\n            16.6,\n            31.3,\n            0.96\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ability_quintile\",\n            1044,\n            1044,\n            91.8,\n            5.3,\n            2.9,\n            1.19\n          ],\n          [\n            \"random_sanity\",\n            955,\n            955,\n            99.1,\n            0.7,\n            0.18,\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: Within-Family Confound Control\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"family_pair\",\n          \"N_ref\",\n          \"N_foc\",\n          \"C_pct\",\n          \"overlap_w_temporal_jaccard\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Llama-2 vs Llama-3\",\n            161,\n            308,\n            61.3,\n            0.41\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mistral vs Mixtral\",\n            89,\n            112,\n            38.7,\n            0.34\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Temporal DIF at 31.3% is the load-bearing finding for Claim 1. The near-zero random-split sanity check (0.18%) and low ability-cohort C% (2.9%) establish that the temporal signal is real and specific to time, not ability. Within-family results (Llama 61.3%, Mistral 38.7%) show DIF persists even within architecture families, suggesting item instability is not purely a between-family artifact. Enrichment≈0.96 directly motivates the ground truth meta-critique (Claim 2).\\n\\n\\n[DONE — actual registry data] 31.3% C% reflects genuine temporal item instability: model generations 2023→2024-H1 differ in training data composition, RLHF procedures, and data curation practices, all of which change how items function. Enrichment≈0.96 against Li et al. labels is expected and predicted: web-overlap labels capture item existence on the internet (global exposure), not whether an item functions differently across cohorts (differential contamination). DIF detects the latter; the two measures are orthogonal by construction. Random-split sanity (0.18%) confirms the 31.3% is not a statistical artifact of the MH procedure. Domain correlation ρ=0.307 is modest — subjects where models improved more show slightly more DIF, but item-level decomposition (plan_019, done separately) confirms this explains at most 7.6% of the instability.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Empirically validate DIF's robustness to RL-trained models. Three cohort compositions (SFT-only, +alignment-RL, +reasoning-RL). Traditional detector comparison (Min-K%, ConStat) on 10-15 reasoning-RL models individually.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_sft_only_models\": 2340,\n      \"n_alignment_rl_models\": 477,\n      \"n_reasoning_rl_models\": 13,\n      \"dif_flag_jaccard_sft_vs_sft_alignrl\": 0.912,\n      \"dif_flag_jaccard_sft_vs_sft_allrl\": 0.874,\n      \"dif_flag_jaccard_alignrl_vs_allrl\": 0.935,\n      \"dif_c_pct_sft_only_cohort\": 31.1,\n      \"dif_c_pct_sft_alignrl_cohort\": 31.5,\n      \"dif_c_pct_sft_allrl_cohort\": 31.8,\n      \"mink_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.563,\n      \"constat_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.614,\n      \"dif_auroc_cohort_with_rl\": 0.831,\n      \"delong_p_dif_vs_mink\": 0.032,\n      \"delong_p_dif_vs_constat\": 0.078,\n      \"tost_equivalence_p\": 0.117,\n      \"bootstrap_auroc_diff_ci_dif_mink\": [\n        0.06,\n        0.33\n      ],\n      \"bootstrap_auroc_diff_ci_dif_constat\": [\n        -0.02,\n        0.28\n      ],\n      \"power_detection_boundary_p\": 0.45,\n      \"power_detection_boundary_delta\": 1.4,\n      \"power_detection_boundary_N\": 80\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A1: DIF Flag Stability Across Cohort Compositions\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"cohort\",\n          \"N_total\",\n          \"C_pct\",\n          \"Jaccard_vs_SFT\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"SFT-only\",\n            2340,\n            31.1,\n            1.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SFT + alignment-RL\",\n            2817,\n            31.5,\n            0.912\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SFT + all-RL\",\n            2830,\n            31.8,\n            0.874\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A2: Per-Model Detector Comparison on Reasoning-RL Models\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"detector\",\n          \"AUROC\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"degradation_vs_nonRL\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Min-K%\",\n            0.563,\n            \"[0.41, 0.72]\",\n            \"-0.31\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ConStat\",\n            0.614,\n            \"[0.46, 0.77]\",\n            \"-0.24\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DIF (group)\",\n            0.831,\n            \"[0.74, 0.92]\",\n            \"-0.02\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Results confirm the theoretical argument: DIF's group-level conditioning makes it robust to RL-induced output distribution changes, while per-model detectors (Min-K%, ConStat) degrade substantially. However, N=13 reasoning-RL models precludes formal equivalence testing. Appendix-appropriate: strengthens Claim 1 narrative without being load-bearing. The power surface shows DIF retains detection for δ≥1.4 at p≥0.45, covering the operationally relevant regime (large effects with moderate exploitation).\\n\\n\\nDIF's group-level design conditions on total score, making it fundamentally insensitive to individual model output distributions. Adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a cohort of N≥80 shifts the score distribution marginally (~0.3pp in C%) because MH's quintile-based matching absorbs ability variation. The high Jaccard (0.87-0.91) reflects that DIF flags are driven by the bulk population, not the RL tail. Min-K% degrades to near-chance (AUROC=0.56) because RL training contracts the NLL gap that Min-K% exploits — the per-token likelihood distribution becomes flatter, destroying the memorization signal. ConStat fares slightly better (0.61) because perturbation sensitivity is partially preserved under alignment-RL but substantially reduced under reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO explicitly optimize for robust reasoning). TOST at p=0.117 is inconclusive, honestly reflecting the N=13 limitation. This is presented in limitations as \\\"suggestive evidence of RL robustness, underpowered for formal equivalence.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation evaluation on MMLU. Generate paraphrases for 200+ items, compare original vs reformulated performance, apply FDR-controlled detection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_paraphrased\": 237,\n      \"semantic_similarity_mean\": 0.918,\n      \"human_quality_pct\": 94.1,\n      \"constat_tpr_non_rl_contaminated\": 0.438,\n      \"constat_tpr_rl_contaminated\": 0.172,\n      \"constat_fpr_fdr_q005\": 0.038,\n      \"cohens_d_non_rl\": 0.871,\n      \"cohens_d_rl\": 0.293,\n      \"perf_drop_contaminated_non_rl_pct\": -13.1,\n      \"perf_drop_contaminated_rl_pct\": -4.2,\n      \"perf_drop_clean_pct\": -2.8,\n      \"paraphrase_difficulty_shift_mean\": 0.04,\n      \"n_models_evaluated\": 48\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A3: ConStat Detection by Model Training Type\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"model_type\",\n          \"N_models\",\n          \"TPR\",\n          \"FPR\",\n          \"Cohen_d\",\n          \"perf_drop_pct\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"SFT-only\",\n            22,\n            0.438,\n            0.038,\n            0.871,\n            -13.1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Alignment-RL\",\n            18,\n            0.172,\n            0.041,\n            0.293,\n            -4.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Clean (no contamination)\",\n            8,\n            \"—\",\n            0.038,\n            \"—\",\n            -2.8\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"ConStat demonstrates moderate detection of surface memorization in non-RL models but degrades substantially for RL models. This characterizes ConStat's complementary detection niche and motivates ensemble approaches. Appendix-appropriate: validates one component of the ensemble framework (Claim 2 old, now dropped to future work) without being needed for current claims.\\n\\n\\nConStat's detection mechanism exploits surface-level memorization: models that memorized exact item phrasings show large performance drops on semantically equivalent paraphrases. Non-RL models exhibit this cleanly (d=0.87, TPR=0.44) because their memorization is token-sequence-specific. RL-trained models show attenuated signal (d=0.29, TPR=0.17) because reward optimization encourages generalization beyond exact training sequences — the RL objective effectively launders memorization into robust capability, making it indistinguishable from genuine learning. FPR at 0.038 confirms FDR control works. The clean-item performance drop (-2.8%) reflects natural difficulty increase from paraphrasing (slight semantic drift, unfamiliar phrasing) rather than contamination. This complements DIF: ConStat detects per-model surface memorization, DIF detects group-level differential functioning regardless of mechanism.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"DVD generation variance evaluation on MMLU. 50 responses per item via temperature sampling on 10-15 open-weight models. Detect bimodal distributions as contamination signal.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_models\": 12,\n      \"n_items_evaluated\": 200,\n      \"n_generations_per_item\": 50,\n      \"temperature\": 0.8,\n      \"dvd_tpr_verbatim\": 0.523,\n      \"dvd_tpr_variant\": 0.138,\n      \"dvd_fpr\": 0.062,\n      \"bimodality_coeff_contaminated\": 0.714,\n      \"bimodality_coeff_clean\": 0.283,\n      \"gen_variance_contaminated_mean\": 0.087,\n      \"gen_variance_contaminated_sd\": 0.131,\n      \"gen_variance_clean_mean\": 0.312,\n      \"gen_variance_clean_sd\": 0.108,\n      \"auroc_verbatim\": 0.813,\n      \"auroc_variant\": 0.571,\n      \"gpu_hours_used\": 52\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A4: DVD Detection by Contamination Type\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"contamination_type\",\n          \"N_items\",\n          \"TPR\",\n          \"FPR\",\n          \"bimodality_coeff\",\n          \"mean_gen_variance\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"verbatim\",\n            80,\n            0.523,\n            0.062,\n            0.714,\n            0.087\n          ],\n          [\n            \"variant\",\n            60,\n            0.138,\n            0.062,\n            0.391,\n            0.224\n          ],\n          [\n            \"clean\",\n            60,\n            \"—\",\n            0.062,\n            0.283,\n            0.312\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"DVD occupies a narrow but clear detection niche: verbatim exact-match memorization. Its inability to detect variant contamination is a fundamental limitation — generation variance cannot distinguish \\\"learned from contaminated data\\\" from \\\"genuinely competent.\\\" This underscores the complementarity argument for ensemble approaches and the advantage of DIF's mechanism-agnostic group-level detection. Appendix-appropriate: useful for characterizing the detection landscape but not needed for current claims.\\n\\n\\nVerbatim memorization produces near-deterministic outputs — the model has effectively learned a lookup table for memorized items, generating the correct answer with probability >0.95 regardless of temperature. This creates bimodal generation distributions (always correct or fully random) with near-zero variance, easily distinguished from clean items (moderate variance reflecting genuine uncertainty). Variant contamination does NOT produce this bimodality because the model learned the underlying concept rather than the exact answer string — generation variance remains moderate, and DVD cannot distinguish \\\"learned from training data\\\" from \\\"genuinely knows.\\\" This limits DVD to a specific niche: detecting exact-match verbatim memorization of item text + answer. Complementary to DIF (group-level, mechanism-agnostic) and ConStat (perturbation-based, per-model).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Majority voting and weighted fusion. Coverage analysis by contamination mechanism.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"dif_alone_tpr\": 0.682,\n      \"constat_alone_tpr\": 0.438,\n      \"dvd_alone_tpr\": 0.523,\n      \"majority_2of3_tpr\": 0.731,\n      \"majority_2of3_fpr\": 0.028,\n      \"majority_2of3_precision\": 0.921,\n      \"weighted_fusion_tpr\": 0.778,\n      \"weighted_fusion_fpr\": 0.057,\n      \"weighted_fusion_precision\": 0.883,\n      \"uniform_weight_tpr\": 0.752,\n      \"uniform_weight_fpr\": 0.044,\n      \"optimal_weights_dif\": 0.43,\n      \"optimal_weights_constat\": 0.32,\n      \"optimal_weights_dvd\": 0.25,\n      \"cv_weight_stability_cv\": 0.13,\n      \"coverage_dif_only_pct\": 22.8,\n      \"coverage_constat_only_pct\": 13.6,\n      \"coverage_dvd_only_pct\": 8.1,\n      \"coverage_multi_signal_pct\": 55.5\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A5: Ensemble Detection Performance\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"strategy\",\n          \"TPR\",\n          \"FPR\",\n          \"precision\",\n          \"F1\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"DIF only\",\n            0.682,\n            0.036,\n            0.892,\n            0.773\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ConStat only\",\n            0.438,\n            0.038,\n            0.854,\n            0.579\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DVD only\",\n            0.523,\n            0.062,\n            0.809,\n            0.635\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2-of-3 majority\",\n            0.731,\n            0.028,\n            0.921,\n            0.815\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Weighted fusion\",\n            0.778,\n            0.057,\n            0.883,\n            0.827\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Uniform weights\",\n            0.752,\n            0.044,\n            0.893,\n            0.817\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble improves coverage but is not needed for current paper claims (Claim 2 pivoted from ensemble to ground truth meta-critique). Results support future work direction: multi-signal contamination detection as a practical pipeline. The 22.8% DIF-only coverage confirms DIF detects contamination modes invisible to per-model methods. Appendix-appropriate: demonstrates feasibility without being load-bearing.\\n\\n\\nThe three detectors exploit orthogonal contamination dimensions: DIF measures differential group-level item functioning (mechanism-agnostic, requires cohort comparison), ConStat measures per-model perturbation sensitivity (targets surface memorization), DVD measures generation variance (targets verbatim memorization). Their error patterns are weakly correlated because they target different aspects of contamination. Majority voting suppresses false positives (FPR=0.028) by requiring convergent evidence from 2+ independent mechanisms. Weighted fusion gains ~5pp TPR over majority voting at the cost of higher FPR (0.057) by allowing strong single-signal evidence to override absent signals. Optimal weights favor DIF (0.43) because it has the broadest coverage (mechanism-agnostic, group-level), followed by ConStat (0.32) and DVD (0.25). The modest improvement over uniform weights reflects limited calibration data.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n    \"description\": \"GSM8K DIF replication (1,319 items × 6,702 models). Semi-synthetic validation. GSM1K contamination gap correlation (N=33).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"step1_n_items\": 1319,\n      \"step1_n_ref\": 1017,\n      \"step1_n_foc\": 804,\n      \"step1_standard_c_pct\": 18.9,\n      \"step1_external_c_pct\": 47.4,\n      \"step1_sanity_c_pct\": 0.3,\n      \"step1_standard_external_spearman\": 0.926,\n      \"step2_standard_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n      \"step2_external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.517,\n      \"step3_n_models\": 33,\n      \"step3_pearson_r\": -0.478,\n      \"step3_pearson_p\": 0.005,\n      \"step3_spearman_rho\": -0.403,\n      \"step3_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n      \"step3_partial_r\": -0.385,\n      \"step3_partial_p\": 0.027,\n      \"step3_bca_ci_pearson\": [\n        -0.714,\n        -0.155\n      ],\n      \"step3_bca_ci_spearman\": [\n        -0.687,\n        -0.031\n      ],\n      \"step3_bca_ci_partial\": [\n        -0.64,\n        -0.058\n      ],\n      \"step3_permutation_p\": 0.014,\n      \"step3_cook_d_max_model\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n      \"step3_leave_one_out_r_range\": [\n        -0.537,\n        -0.374\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: GSM8K DIF Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"matching\",\n          \"C_pct\",\n          \"sanity_pct\",\n          \"ΔTPR_p10\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"standard (total score)\",\n            18.9,\n            0.3,\n            0.818\n          ],\n          [\n            \"external (cross-benchmark)\",\n            47.4,\n            \"—\",\n            0.517\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: GSM1K Contamination Gap Correlation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"statistic\",\n          \"value\",\n          \"p\",\n          \"95% BCa CI\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Pearson r\",\n            -0.478,\n            0.005,\n            \"[-0.714, -0.155]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spearman ρ\",\n            -0.403,\n            0.02,\n            \"[-0.687, -0.031]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Partial r\",\n            -0.385,\n            0.027,\n            \"[-0.640, -0.058]\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"GSM8K replication confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (Claim 3). GSM1K anti-correlation provides the fourth layer of Claim 4 (downstream impact). Under best tier, reviewers treat r=−0.478 as suggestive-but-consistent rather than attacking it as underpowered — the convergent evidence (all CIs exclude zero, permutation p=0.014, leave-one-out stable) is sufficient for a discovery paper. Plan_017 redundancy result (ρ≈0.997) independently supports Claim 4 even if Step 3 is discounted.\\n\\n\\n[DONE — actual registry data] GSM8K temporal DIF at 18.9% (vs MMLU 31.3%) reflects GSM8K's more homogeneous construct (single-domain math vs 57-subject MMLU) — fewer dimensions of variation means fewer items whose functioning changes across generations. Semi-synthetic ΔTPR=+0.818 confirms the detection framework ports to single-domain benchmarks with high power. Step 3 anti-correlation (r=−0.478) is the key downstream finding: models with larger GSM8K-GSM1K contamination gaps show disproportionately LOWER performance on DIF-C items. This is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items (memorized items that function uniformly across cohorts), while DIF items capture genuine capability differences that contamination cannot inflate. All BCa CIs exclude zero, and leave-one-out analysis confirms robustness to individual influential models. N=33 is honestly acknowledged as suggestive — the finding is consistent but a larger matched sample would strengthen it.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n    \"description\": \"Bifactor IRT model on METABENCH MMLU (general + 57 subject factors). Anchor item selection. Fixed-parameter equating pilot.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"general_factor_variance_pct\": 58.3,\n      \"subject_specific_variance_mean_pct\": 30.8,\n      \"residual_variance_pct\": 10.9,\n      \"bifactor_rmsea\": 0.019,\n      \"bifactor_cfi\": 0.953,\n      \"bifactor_tli\": 0.941,\n      \"unidim_rmsea\": 0.035,\n      \"unidim_cfi\": 0.882,\n      \"unidim_tli\": 0.864,\n      \"n_anchor_items\": 5640,\n      \"anchor_criteria\": \"no DIF, rpbis>0.20, general loading>0.30\",\n      \"within_subject_dif_after_bifactor_pct\": 35.8,\n      \"within_subject_dif_unidim_pct\": 38.1,\n      \"dif_reduction_from_bifactor_pp\": 2.3,\n      \"equating_rho_general\": 0.961,\n      \"equating_rho_subject_range\": [\n        0.891,\n        0.974\n      ],\n      \"equating_rho_subject_below_095\": 8,\n      \"convergence_em_iterations\": 2140,\n      \"convergence_achieved\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A6: Model Fit Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"model\",\n          \"RMSEA\",\n          \"CFI\",\n          \"TLI\",\n          \"n_params\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Unidimensional\",\n            0.035,\n            0.882,\n            0.864,\n            25016\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Bifactor (1+57)\",\n            0.019,\n            0.953,\n            0.941,\n            37524\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A7: DIF Persistence After Bifactor Conditioning\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"conditioning\",\n          \"within_subject_C_pct\",\n          \"reduction_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"None (unidimensional)\",\n            38.1,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Bifactor general+specific\",\n            35.8,\n            2.3\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Bifactor's 2.3pp DIF reduction (~6% relative) confirms that >90% of temporal DIF is genuine item-level instability, not dimensional misfit artifact. This is the strongest possible evidence for Claim 1's robustness to IRT model specification concerns. The equating pilot demonstrates feasibility but remains below the quality bar for a main-body claim — consistent with paper_framing's decision to relegate this to appendix. The fit improvement (RMSEA halved) is itself valuable: it confirms the diagnosis that 98% |Lz|>2 reflects multidimensionality, not fundamental model invalidity.\\n\\n\\nBifactor correctly identifies MMLU's multidimensional structure — 57 subjects create substantial specific factors (31% of variance). However, DIF persists at 35.8% even after extracting subject-specific variance (only 2.3pp reduction from 38.1%), confirming that the instability is genuinely item-level, not an artifact of dimensional confounding. The general factor dominates (58%) because MMLU subjects share a common \\\"general LLM ability\\\" axis — models good at one subject tend to be good at others. IRT fit improves substantially (RMSEA 0.035→0.019, CFI 0.88→0.95), validating that multidimensionality was the primary source of unidimensional misfit, but this improved fit does not resolve DIF. Equating at ρ=0.961 on the general factor demonstrates feasibility of anchor-based score maintenance, but 8/57 subjects fall below ρ=0.95 (small-N subjects with <50 items where anchor selection is constrained). This supports future work on benchmark maintenance but is appropriately appendix material — the finding that bifactor doesn't resolve DIF is itself evidence for Claim 1's robustness.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n    \"description\": \"LLM-based replacement item generation for DIF-flagged MMLU items. Prompt-based vs student-simulation difficulty estimation. Holdout validation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_generated\": 500,\n      \"n_target_items\": 250,\n      \"candidates_per_target\": 2,\n      \"difficulty_rho_prompt\": 0.362,\n      \"difficulty_rho_simulation\": 0.731,\n      \"difficulty_rho_best_candidate\": 0.793,\n      \"construct_alignment_expert_pct\": 88.7,\n      \"discrimination_rpbis_rho\": 0.514,\n      \"holdout_within_015_difficulty_pct\": 83.6,\n      \"distractor_quality_plausible_pct\": 76.2,\n      \"simulation_panel_n_models\": 30,\n      \"gpu_hours_used\": 164\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A8: Difficulty Estimation Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"ρ_with_target\",\n          \"MAE_difficulty\",\n          \"coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Prompt-based targeting\",\n            0.362,\n            0.183,\n            \"100%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Student simulation (30 models)\",\n            0.731,\n            0.094,\n            \"100%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Best of 2 candidates\",\n            0.793,\n            0.078,\n            \"100%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The prompt→simulation gap (ρ=0.36→0.73) is the key practical finding: empirical calibration is essential for item replacement pipelines. Appendix-appropriate: supports future benchmark regeneration work but is below the quality bar for a main-body claim (consistent with paper_framing's decision to drop regeneration from claims). The 76% distractor quality suggests human review remains necessary for production deployment.\\n\\n\\nLLMs generate on-topic items reliably (89% construct alignment) because they understand MMLU subject boundaries and question formats. However, prompt-based difficulty targeting fails (ρ=0.36) because the generator lacks fine-grained knowledge of what makes an item hard for OTHER LLMs — difficulty is an emergent property of item-population interaction, not an intrinsic text feature. Student simulation (using 30 diverse models as examinees) provides empirical difficulty estimates that capture actual model behavior, achieving ρ=0.73 — approaching the r=0.75-0.82 reported in the student simulation literature (arXiv 2601.09953). Best-candidate selection from 2 options improves to ρ=0.79 by choosing the candidate closer to target difficulty. Distractor quality (76%) is the weakest link: generating plausible wrong answers for multiple-choice items requires understanding common misconceptions, which LLMs model imperfectly. This finding supports the conclusion that automated item generation is feasible but requires empirical calibration — prompt-based difficulty targeting alone is insufficient.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"Bayesian cumulative DIF priors across 4 simulated benchmark administrations. Power accumulation for medium effects (δ=1.0).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_administrations\": 4,\n      \"n_per_admin\": 80,\n      \"delta_simulated\": 1.0,\n      \"individual_power_mean\": 0.415,\n      \"individual_power_range\": [\n        0.398,\n        0.432\n      ],\n      \"cumulative_power_after_2\": 0.576,\n      \"cumulative_power_after_3\": 0.718,\n      \"cumulative_power_after_4\": 0.812,\n      \"fpr_across_accumulations_max\": 0.048,\n      \"bf10_after_1\": 2.14,\n      \"bf10_after_2\": 4.83,\n      \"bf10_after_3\": 9.27,\n      \"bf10_after_4\": 16.7,\n      \"bf10_moderate_evidence_threshold\": 3.0,\n      \"power_at_bf10_gt3_after_2\": 0.648,\n      \"mh_individual_power_at_delta10\": 0.383,\n      \"power_gain_cumulative_vs_individual\": \"+0.303 (after 3)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A9: Cumulative Bayesian Power by Administration\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"n_admins\",\n          \"cumulative_N\",\n          \"power\",\n          \"BF10\",\n          \"FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            80,\n            0.415,\n            2.14,\n            0.042\n          ],\n          [\n            2,\n            160,\n            0.576,\n            4.83,\n            0.044\n          ],\n          [\n            3,\n            240,\n            0.718,\n            9.27,\n            0.046\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            320,\n            0.812,\n            16.7,\n            0.048\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Simulation-only result (no real data). Confirms theoretical prediction that Bayesian cumulative DIF closes the power gap for medium effects. The practical implication: benchmark monitoring programs can detect δ=1.0 DIF after 3 waves of ~80 models each, even though no single wave has adequate power. Appendix-appropriate: methodological contribution for future longitudinal monitoring, not needed for current claims. Underdeveloped for main-body inclusion — needs real multi-wave data to validate.\\n\\n\\nIndividual MH tests at N=80, δ=1.0 have insufficient power (~0.42) — this is the \\\"power gap\\\" identified in phase 2 simulations (pass criterion required δ≥1.4). Bayesian updating converts individually inconclusive tests into cumulative evidence: the posterior from each administration constrains the prior for the next. After 3 administrations, cumulative power crosses 0.70 (the conventional adequate-power threshold) because the accumulated evidence from 3×80=240 effective observations exceeds what any single N=80 test provides. The Bayes factor trajectory (2.1→4.8→9.3→16.7) follows the expected log-linear accumulation under consistent DIF. FPR remains controlled (≤0.048) because the Bayesian framework naturally penalizes inconsistent evidence — items with spurious DIF in one administration but not others see their posterior regress toward the null. This addresses the practical concern that benchmark monitoring requires repeated measurement, not one-shot detection.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Progressive item replacement validity analysis. Ranking stability, score reliability, construct coverage as function of replacement rate (5-50%).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"replacement_rates\": [\n        0.05,\n        0.1,\n        0.2,\n        0.31,\n        0.5\n      ],\n      \"rho_at_005\": 0.9993,\n      \"rho_at_010\": 0.9984,\n      \"rho_at_020\": 0.9968,\n      \"rho_at_031\": 0.9951,\n      \"rho_at_050\": 0.9912,\n      \"alpha_full\": 0.9937,\n      \"alpha_at_031\": 0.9882,\n      \"alpha_at_050\": 0.9791,\n      \"construct_coverage_at_031\": \"57/57\",\n      \"construct_coverage_at_050\": \"56/57\",\n      \"construct_coverage_threshold_subjects_below_5items\": 0.62,\n      \"validity_threshold_rho_lt_099\": 0.453,\n      \"shr_full\": 0.9934,\n      \"shr_at_031\": 0.9881,\n      \"shr_at_050\": 0.9763,\n      \"replacement_strategy\": \"DIF-flagged items replaced by matched-difficulty anchors\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A10: Validity Degradation Under Progressive Replacement\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"replacement_pct\",\n          \"ρ_ranking\",\n          \"α_reliability\",\n          \"SHR\",\n          \"construct_coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            1.0,\n            0.994,\n            0.993,\n            \"57/57\"\n          ],\n          [\n            5,\n            0.999,\n            0.993,\n            0.993,\n            \"57/57\"\n          ],\n          [\n            10,\n            0.998,\n            0.992,\n            0.992,\n            \"57/57\"\n          ],\n          [\n            20,\n            0.997,\n            0.99,\n            0.99,\n            \"57/57\"\n          ],\n          [\n            31,\n            0.995,\n            0.988,\n            0.988,\n            \"57/57\"\n          ],\n          [\n            50,\n            0.991,\n            0.979,\n            0.976,\n            \"56/57\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Directly supports the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing: replacing 31% of items (all DIF-flagged) preserves >99.5% of ranking information, demonstrating massive measurement redundancy. The convex degradation curve means benchmark maintainers have large headroom for item updates. Appendix-appropriate: clean supplementary result that reinforces Claim 4 (redundancy layer) without adding new claims.\\n\\n\\nMMLU's massive redundancy (12,508 items, most subjects with 100+ items) provides enormous buffering against item replacement. Ranking stability at 31% replacement (ρ=0.9951) closely matches plan_017's DIF removal result (ρ=0.997) — the slight discrepancy reflects calibration noise in replacement items vs clean removal. The curve is convex: initial replacement costs almost nothing (ρ=0.9993 at 5%) because replaced items are drawn from the unstable pool (high DIF, contributing noise rather than signal). The validity threshold (ρ<0.99) is not crossed until ~45% replacement, demonstrating that even aggressive maintenance preserves benchmark validity. Construct coverage holds at 57/57 subjects through 31% because DIF items are distributed across subjects (not clustered in a few), so no subject loses all its items. Score reliability drops only 0.55pp (0.994→0.988) at 31% because Cronbach's α is driven by total item count and inter-item correlation, both of which remain high after replacement.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n    \"description\": \"Item-Focused Trees with continuous release date covariate. Compare detection power and flexibility vs categorical MH-DIF.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_models_with_release_date\": 3412,\n      \"ift_significant_items_pct\": 33.8,\n      \"ift_mh_overlap_jaccard\": 0.762,\n      \"ift_only_items_pct\": 8.3,\n      \"mh_only_items_pct\": 5.2,\n      \"natural_breakpoints\": [\n        \"2023-03-15\",\n        \"2023-08-22\",\n        \"2024-01-10\"\n      ],\n      \"n_terminal_nodes\": 4,\n      \"release_date_variable_importance\": 0.71,\n      \"param_count_importance\": 0.14,\n      \"training_data_size_importance\": 0.09,\n      \"mean_dif_early_2023\": 0.82,\n      \"mean_dif_late_2023\": 1.21,\n      \"mean_dif_early_2024\": 1.48,\n      \"dif_magnitude_release_date_r\": 0.391,\n      \"gradual_drift_items_pct\": 8.3,\n      \"sharp_transition_items_pct\": 5.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A11: IFT vs MH-DIF Detection Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"significant_items_pct\",\n          \"unique_items_pct\",\n          \"natural_breakpoints\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MH categorical\",\n            31.3,\n            5.2,\n            \"2023 vs 2024-H1 (manual)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"IFT continuous\",\n            33.8,\n            8.3,\n            \"Mar-23, Aug-23, Jan-24 (data-driven)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table A12: IFT Variable Importance\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"covariate\",\n          \"importance\",\n          \"interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"release_date\",\n            0.71,\n            \"temporal position drives DIF\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"param_count\",\n            0.14,\n            \"larger models show slightly different DIF\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"training_data_size\",\n            0.09,\n            \"marginal contribution\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"IFT provides a complementary lens: confirms the temporal DIF finding is robust to the choice of categorical split point, and reveals gradual drift items missed by binary comparison. The natural breakpoints are empirically interesting (potential link to model release waves) but not developed into a claim. Appendix-appropriate: robustness check that strengthens Claim 1 without adding complexity to the main narrative.\\n\\n\\nIFT treats release date as a continuous covariate, revealing temporal DIF structure that categorical splits can miss. The 76% Jaccard overlap with MH confirms that the binary 2023/2024-H1 split captures most of the signal — the categorical approximation is adequate for the primary analysis. The additional 8.3% IFT-only items are \\\"gradual drifters\\\" whose functioning changes smoothly across the timeline without a single dramatic shift — these are invisible to any binary split but accumulate meaningful DIF over the full period. Natural breakpoints (March 2023, August 2023, January 2024) likely correspond to major model release waves (Llama-2 era, post-ChatGPT proliferation, GPT-4 derivatives). The monotonic DIF magnitude increase (0.82→1.21→1.48) confirms genuine temporal trend rather than categorical artifact. Release date dominates variable importance (0.71) over model size (0.14) — WHEN matters more than HOW BIG, consistent with training data composition / contamination explaining instability rather than raw capability scaling.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"DIF within decoder-only-only subpopulation. Test whether architecture heterogeneity removal improves IRT fit and reduces spurious DIF.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_models\": 5227,\n      \"decoder_only_models\": 5226,\n      \"decoder_only_pct\": 99.98,\n      \"non_decoder_models\": 1,\n      \"c_pct_full\": 31.3,\n      \"c_pct_decoder_only\": 31.3,\n      \"jaccard_full_vs_decoder\": 1.0,\n      \"irt_rmsea_decoder_only\": 0.033,\n      \"irt_rmsea_full\": 0.034,\n      \"lz_gt2_pct_decoder_only\": 97.9,\n      \"lz_gt2_pct_full\": 98.0,\n      \"delta_mh_correlation_full_vs_decoder\": 0.9999\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Architecture Stratification\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"population\",\n          \"N\",\n          \"C_pct\",\n          \"RMSEA\",\n          \"|Lz|>2 pct\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Full\",\n            5227,\n            31.3,\n            0.034,\n            \"98.0%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Decoder-only\",\n            5226,\n            31.3,\n            0.033,\n            \"97.9%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the most decisive robustness check in the paper: the architecture confound concern is dissolved empirically rather than argued away theoretically. Main-body appropriate as part of Claim 1 robustness (§4). The finding that the LLM ecosystem is architecturally homogeneous has broader implications for psychometric analyses of LLM benchmarks — the \\\"examinee population\\\" is far more homogeneous than human testing populations, which simplifies DIF analysis but raises questions about construct validity.\\n\\n\\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227), reflecting the empirical reality that virtually all LLMs evaluated on MMLU since 2023 are autoregressive transformers. The single non-decoder model has zero statistical influence on any group-level analysis — removing it produces Jaccard=1.0 because MH chi-squared tests are robust to single-observation perturbations in large samples. IRT fit does not improve (RMSEA 0.034→0.033) because the misfit (98% |Lz|>2) is driven by MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity. This result definitively dissolves the worst-case reviewer concern about architecture confounding. The architecture homogeneity is itself an empirical finding: the LLM evaluation ecosystem has converged on decoder-only transformers, making architecture-based stratification unnecessary for this population. Future benchmarks with more diverse architectures (e.g., state-space models, hybrid architectures) would need to revisit this analysis.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into real METABENCH response matrix. Measure DIF detection power under controlled ground truth. Vary injection parameters.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_external_tpr_p0\": 0.326,\n      \"temporal_external_tpr_p03\": 0.548,\n      \"temporal_external_tpr_p05\": 0.834,\n      \"temporal_external_tpr_p07\": 0.99,\n      \"temporal_external_tpr_p10\": 1.0,\n      \"temporal_external_fpr\": 0.314,\n      \"temporal_external_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n      \"temporal_external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n      \"cross_subject_delta_fpr\": 0.0,\n      \"within_subject_fpr_inflation_p10\": 0.186,\n      \"ability_external_tpr_p10\": 0.8,\n      \"ability_external_fpr\": 0.493,\n      \"dose_response_monotonic\": true,\n      \"matching_variable_contamination_confirmed\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Semi-Synthetic Detection Power (Cross-Subject Matching)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"exploitation_p\",\n          \"TPR\",\n          \"ΔTPR\",\n          \"FPR\",\n          \"ΔFPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            0.0,\n            0.326,\n            0.0,\n            0.314,\n            0.0\n          ],\n          [\n            0.3,\n            0.548,\n            0.222,\n            0.314,\n            0.0\n          ],\n          [\n            0.5,\n            0.834,\n            0.508,\n            0.314,\n            0.0\n          ],\n          [\n            0.7,\n            0.99,\n            0.664,\n            0.314,\n            0.0\n          ],\n          [\n            1.0,\n            1.0,\n            0.674,\n            0.314,\n            0.0\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Matching Variable Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"matching\",\n          \"ΔFPR_p10\",\n          \"ΔTPR_p05\",\n          \"FPR_inflation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Within-subject\",\n            0.186,\n            \"varies\",\n            \"YES\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cross-subject\",\n            0.0,\n            0.508,\n            \"NO\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the primary validation for Claim 3 (semi-synthetic framework) and provides controlled ground truth addressing the circularity concern in Claim 2. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is the key methodological contribution — it solves the matching variable contamination problem that plagues within-subject DIF when the conditioning variable is endogenous. Main-body material for §4.\\n\\n[DONE — actual registry data] Semi-synthetic injection into real response matrices provides the controlled ground truth that proxy labels cannot. ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 demonstrates that DIF detects differential contamination when the exploitation rate is moderate — this is the core validation for the detection claim. The dose-response curve (p=0.3→0.5→0.7→1.0 maps to ΔTPR=0.22→0.51→0.66→0.67) confirms detection power scales with contamination intensity, as expected from the DIF effect size formula (Δ_MH ∝ log odds ratio of correct response, which increases with exploitation probability). Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000) because the matching variable (total score on OTHER subjects) is causally independent of contamination injected into a specific subject. Within-subject matching shows FPR inflation (+0.186) because contaminating items in a subject inflates total score on that subject, which contaminates the matching variable. This distinction is a subtle but general methodological insight: any DIF application where the matching variable can be affected by the grouping variable will show FPR inflation.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":996,"startLine":1,"totalLines":996}}}
