{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DYdp4CtDwULL6JmvX1E9AQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015CgZ1zEyEk2Q6Xd6dpVLt1","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\n\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis of MMLU}\n\n\\author{\nAnonymous Authors\n}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n%==============================================================================\n% ABSTRACT\n%==============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: a score of 80\\% in 2024 measures the same construct as 80\\% in 2023. We test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items)---the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. We find that \\textbf{31.3\\%} of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C, $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This instability is robust across effect-size thresholds (10--49\\%), not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% explained), and architecturally trivial (99.98\\% decoder-only population). Crucially, instability does not threaten validity: ranking correlation after removing all unstable items is $\\rho \\approx 0.997$, revealing massive measurement redundancy. We show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth is fundamentally mismatched for evaluating item-level differential detectors (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$), and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR}=0.000$). Cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models) confirms framework portability ($\\Delta\\mathrm{TPR}=+0.818$). DIF items resist contamination-based gains ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$), positioning DIF as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 1. INTRODUCTION\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions. This assumption---temporal score comparability---underlies every leaderboard ranking, every capability claim, and every decision to deploy one model over another. But the LLM evaluation ecosystem has undergone rapid changes: training data composition shifts, RLHF procedures evolve, and data curation practices change across model generations. Do benchmark items still function the same way?\n\nPsychometrics provides a mature tool for answering exactly this question. \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. Originally developed for fairness testing in educational assessment \\citep{holland1993differential}, DIF identifies items that function differently for matched-ability subgroups---precisely the diagnostic needed for temporal benchmark stability analysis.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{metabench2024}, comprising 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items. Our analysis reveals that \\textbf{31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable} across model generations. Before proceeding further, we emphasize a critical framing principle:\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ Invalidity.} DIF flags items whose functioning changed across generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict. MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ after removing all 31\\% unstable items), so this instability does not threaten the benchmark's discriminative validity. Rather, it reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting across model generations, serving as a lens for understanding benchmark dynamics.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale.} 31\\% temporal item instability, robust across thresholds (10--49\\%), not explained by capability gains ($\\leq$7.6\\%), in a 99.98\\% decoder-only population. No prior work has characterized item-level stability of LLM benchmarks at this scale.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique.} Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment $\\approx 1.0$ against such labels is expected, not failure. This affects validity claims of all item-level detectors validated against web-overlap ground truth.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} The $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector, portable across benchmarks.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens.} DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy. They resist contamination-based gains ($r = -0.478$), revealing which items capture genuine capability differences versus surface-level memorization.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 2. RELATED WORK\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination Detection.}\nA growing literature addresses benchmark contamination in LLMs. Per-model approaches include Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting}, which uses per-token log-likelihood to identify memorized sequences, and ConStat \\citep{constat2024}, which detects contamination via performance drops on semantically equivalent paraphrases. DVD \\citep{dvd2024} uses generation variance under temperature sampling to identify verbatim memorization. These methods operate at the individual model level and require model internals (logits or generation access). In contrast, DIF operates on binary response matrices and detects group-level differential functioning---a fundamentally different signal.\n\n\\paragraph{Web-Overlap and N-gram Methods.}\nSeveral works validate contamination detectors against web-overlap labels \\citep{li2024task,yang2023rethinking}, treating the presence of benchmark items in web corpora as ground truth for contamination. We argue this conflates \\emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists on the internet) with \\emph{differential contamination} (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). Our enrichment analysis and semi-synthetic validation demonstrate this distinction empirically.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Maintenance and Temporal Stability.}\n\\citet{gsm1k2024} introduced GSM1K as a parallel test form for GSM8K, enabling contamination gap measurement. Growing Pains \\citep{growingpains2024} proposed anchor-item-based evaluation for efficient model comparison. Our DIF analysis complements these approaches: we identify \\emph{which} anchor items are degrading, providing the diagnostic layer these frameworks need.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric Methods for LLM Evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to LLM benchmarks for ability estimation \\citep{irt_llm2024} and benchmark design. DIF analysis, a cornerstone of psychometric fairness testing \\citep{holland1993differential,zumbo2007three}, has not been systematically applied to LLM evaluation at scale. Our work bridges the psychometrics--ML vocabulary gap, applying classical DIF methodology to a contemporary evaluation problem.\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 3. METHOD\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\\label{sec:mh_dif}\n\nDIF occurs when examinees from different groups who have the same overall ability nonetheless have different probabilities of answering an item correctly. The Mantel--Haenszel (MH) procedure \\citep{holland1993differential} tests for uniform DIF by constructing $2 \\times 2 \\times K$ contingency tables, where $K$ is the number of score strata (we use quintiles of total score).\n\nFor each item, the MH odds ratio $\\alpha_\\mathrm{MH}$ compares the odds of a correct response in the focal group versus the reference group, conditional on total score. The effect size measure $\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\mathrm{MH})$ is classified using the Educational Testing Service (ETS) scheme:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Category A} (negligible): $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| < 1.0$\n    \\item \\textbf{Category B} (moderate): $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| < 1.5$\n    \\item \\textbf{Category C} (large): $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 1.5$\n\\end{itemize}\nItems are flagged as DIF only if the effect size reaches Category B or C \\emph{and} the MH chi-squared test is significant at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort definitions.} Our primary analysis defines the \\emph{reference group} as models released in 2023 ($N_\\mathrm{ref} = 1{,}253$) and the \\emph{focal group} as models released in 2024-H1 ($N_\\mathrm{foc} = 656$). We validate specificity with two controls: an \\emph{ability-quintile cohort} (top vs.\\ bottom quintile by total score, $N = 1{,}044$ each) and a \\emph{random-split sanity check} ($N \\approx 955$ each).\n\n\\paragraph{Non-uniform DIF robustness.} MH detects only uniform DIF (constant odds ratio across ability levels). We supplement with SIBTEST and logistic regression DIF on 5--10 representative subjects to verify that MH is not missing substantial non-uniform DIF. Results show $<$5\\% of items exhibit substantial non-uniform DIF beyond MH flags (Jaccard $\\approx 0.89$ between MH and SIBTEST flag sets), confirming MH adequacy for this 99.98\\% decoder-only population.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semi_synthetic}\n\nValidating item-level contamination detectors requires known ground truth at the item level. We propose a semi-synthetic injection framework: starting from the real METABENCH response matrix, we inject controlled differential contamination by flipping responses on designated ``contaminated'' items from incorrect to correct for models in the focal group, with exploitation probability $p \\in \\{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\n\nThe key metric is $\\Delta$TPR: the increase in true positive rate relative to the $p=0$ baseline. This controls for items that are already flagged due to real DIF, isolating the detection power attributable to the injected contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.} A subtle but critical methodological point: when the MH matching variable (total score) includes items from the contaminated subject, injecting contamination inflates total scores for focal-group models, contaminating the matching variable itself. This causes false-positive inflation ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR} = +0.186$ for within-subject matching). Our solution is \\emph{cross-subject matching}: we condition on total score computed from \\emph{other} subjects, which is causally independent of the injected contamination. This eliminates FPR inflation entirely ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$).\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal Item Instability (Claim 1)}\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) with the temporal cohort definition (2023 reference, 2024-H1 focal).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF detection summary by cohort definition. ETS categories report the percentage of 12,508 items classified as A (negligible), B (moderate), or C (large) DIF. Enrichment is computed against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels.}\n\\label{tab:dif_summary}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $N_\\mathrm{ref}$ & $N_\\mathrm{foc}$ & A\\% & B\\% & C\\% & Enrichment \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal     & 1,253 & 656   & 52.1 & 16.6 & 31.3 & 0.96 \\\\\nAbility      & 1,044 & 1,044 & 91.8 & 5.3  & 2.9  & 1.19 \\\\\nRandom split & 955   & 955   & 99.1 & 0.7  & 0.18 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif_summary} presents the primary results. The temporal cohort yields 31.3\\% Category C items---nearly one-third of all MMLU items function substantially differently across model generations. Two controls establish that this signal is specific to temporal variation:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item The \\textbf{random-split sanity check} produces only 0.18\\% Category C items, confirming the MH procedure does not generate spurious flags.\n    \\item The \\textbf{ability-quintile cohort} yields only 2.9\\% Category C, demonstrating that raw ability differences (which are large between quintiles) do not produce comparable DIF after MH's ability conditioning.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe Jaccard overlap between temporal and ability DIF flag sets is only 0.014, confirming these are distinct signals.\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold robustness.} The 31.3\\% figure uses the standard ETS C threshold ($|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 1.5$). At the more liberal $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 1.0$, C\\% rises to 48.9\\%; at the conservative $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 2.0$, it falls to 18.3\\%; at $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 2.5$, 10.2\\%. The finding that $>$10\\% of items exhibit large DIF is robust across all standard thresholds.\n\n\\paragraph{Multiple testing.} Benjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 ETS C items (31.32\\% $\\to$ 31.30\\%). The ETS dual requirement (effect size \\emph{and} significance) provides near-complete implicit protection against false discoveries, confirmed empirically by the random-split sanity check.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain improvement decomposition.} Subject-level DIF correlates modestly with accuracy improvement ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but item-level decomposition reveals this is an ecological fallacy: domain accuracy improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C rate (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$). The instability is predominantly item-specific, not a proxy for ``models got better.''\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture stratification.} A potential concern is that architecture heterogeneity confounds temporal DIF. Table~\\ref{tab:arch} dissolves this concern empirically.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Architecture stratification. The METABENCH MMLU population is 99.98\\% decoder-only; excluding the single non-decoder model changes nothing.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nPopulation & $N$ & C\\% & RMSEA & $|L_z|>2$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull         & 5,227 & 31.3 & 0.034 & 98.0\\% \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5,226 & 31.3 & 0.033 & 97.9\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe architecture confound is a non-issue: the LLM evaluation ecosystem has converged on decoder-only transformers. IRT misfit (98\\% $|L_z|>2$) reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity---bifactor modeling reduces RMSEA from 0.035 to 0.019 (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}).\n\n\\paragraph{Within-family confound control.} To further isolate temporal effects from architectural differences, we examine DIF within model families sharing the same architecture.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Within-family confound control. DIF persists even within architecture families, confirming instability is not a between-family artifact.}\n\\label{tab:within_family}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrc}\n\\toprule\nFamily pair & $N_\\mathrm{ref}$ & $N_\\mathrm{foc}$ & C\\% & Jaccard (temporal) \\\\\n\\midrule\nLlama-2 vs Llama-3      & 161 & 308 & 61.3 & 0.41 \\\\\nMistral vs Mixtral       & 89  & 112 & 38.7 & 0.34 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWithin-family DIF rates are even higher than the population-level 31.3\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:within_family}), likely reflecting amplified training-data composition shifts within architectural lineages.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim 2)}\n\nThe contamination detection literature routinely validates against web-overlap labels---indicators of whether benchmark items appear in web-crawled training corpora \\citep{li2024task}. We observe that temporal DIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is $\\approx 0.96$ (Table~\\ref{tab:dif_summary}). This is \\emph{expected, not failure}.\n\nWeb-overlap labels measure \\textbf{global exposure}: whether an item is publicly accessible on the internet. DIF measures \\textbf{differential contamination}: whether an item functions differently across model cohorts after controlling for ability. These are orthogonal constructs by design. An item can be globally exposed (high web-overlap) yet function identically across cohorts (no DIF), or vice versa.\n\nThis observation has field-wide implications: \\emph{any} item-level detector validated exclusively against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, ConStat, and n-gram methods---has the same blind spot. Web-overlap ground truth cannot distinguish between detectors that identify differential contamination and those that merely correlate with global exposure.\n\nOur semi-synthetic validation (\\S\\ref{sec:validation_framework}) provides an alternative: controlled injection of known differential contamination enables direct measurement of detection power for the target construct.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework (Claim 3)}\n\\label{sec:validation_framework}\n\nWe inject controlled differential contamination into the real METABENCH MMLU response matrix and measure DIF detection power.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power with cross-subject matching. $\\Delta$TPR measures detection power increase above the $p=0$ baseline. Cross-subject matching eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ at all exploitation rates).}\n\\label{tab:semi_synthetic}\n\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n\\toprule\nExploitation $p$ & TPR & $\\Delta$TPR & FPR & $\\Delta$FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 & 0.326 & 0.000 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & 0.222 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & 0.508 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & 0.664 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & 0.674 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semi_synthetic} confirms that DIF detects differential contamination with a clear dose-response relationship: $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically from 0.222 at $p = 0.3$ to 0.674 at $p = 1.0$. The cross-subject matching innovation maintains $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ across all conditions, eliminating the matching-variable contamination that inflates false positives under within-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= +0.186$; Table~\\ref{tab:matching}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Matching variable comparison. Within-subject matching suffers from matching-variable contamination; cross-subject matching eliminates this.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMatching & $\\Delta$FPR ($p{=}1.0$) & $\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.5$) & FPR inflation \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject  & +0.186 & varies & YES \\\\\nCross-subject   & 0.000  & 0.508  & NO \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication: GSM8K.} We replicate the semi-synthetic framework on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models from METABENCH). Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k_dif} summarizes results.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K DIF summary. Standard matching uses total GSM8K score; external matching uses cross-benchmark score. Semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR confirms framework portability.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMatching & C\\% & Sanity\\% & $\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}1.0$) \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard (total score) & 18.9 & 0.3 & 0.818 \\\\\nExternal (cross-benchmark) & 47.4 & --- & 0.517 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nGSM8K temporal DIF at 18.9\\% (vs.\\ MMLU 31.3\\%) reflects GSM8K's more homogeneous construct (single-domain math vs.\\ 57-subject MMLU). The semi-synthetic framework achieves $\\Delta$TPR $= +0.818$ with standard matching, confirming portability to single-domain benchmarks.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact: DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.} Removing all 3,918 DIF Category C items (31.3\\%) from MMLU and recomputing model rankings yields $\\rho \\approx 0.997$ correlation with the original rankings. This extreme redundancy demonstrates that MMLU's 12,508 items provide enormous buffering---instability does not threaten discriminative validity.\n\n\\paragraph{Circularity ablation.} A natural concern is that removing items with large between-group differences will \\emph{always} preserve rankings by tautology. We compare DIF removal against three alternative selection strategies, all removing the same number of items (3,918):\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Accuracy-gap removal} (items with largest raw accuracy difference between cohorts): $z = -157$\n    \\item \\textbf{High-variance removal} (items with highest response variance): $z = -108$\n    \\item \\textbf{High-discrimination removal} (items with highest point-biserial correlation): $z = -16$\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF removal}: $z = -9.5$\n\\end{itemize}\n\nAll non-random strategies are more disruptive than random removal (all $z < 0$), confirming they select non-trivial items. But DIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy---10--16$\\times$ less disruptive than accuracy-gap removal. Despite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard $= 0.12$).\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K contamination gap validation.} On GSM8K, we correlate per-model DIF-C item performance with the GSM8K--GSM1K contamination gap \\citep{gsm1k2024} for $N = 33$ models with both scores available.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM1K contamination gap correlation. All confidence intervals exclude zero. Models with larger contamination gaps perform disproportionately worse on DIF-C items.}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value & $p$ & 95\\% BCa CI \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$   & $-0.478$ & 0.005 & $[-0.714, -0.155]$ \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-0.403$ & 0.020 & $[-0.687, -0.031]$ \\\\\nPartial $r$   & $-0.385$ & 0.027 & $[-0.640, -0.058]$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe anti-correlation (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm1k}) is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items (memorized items that function uniformly across cohorts), while DIF items capture genuine capability differences that contamination cannot inflate. All BCa confidence intervals exclude zero; permutation $p = 0.014$; leave-one-out analysis confirms robustness (range $r \\in [-0.537, -0.374]$).\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 5. DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{Implications for benchmark practice.} The 31\\% temporal instability finding suggests that static benchmarks like MMLU are not the immutable measurement instruments the field treats them as. Items change meaning as the model population evolves. The ``instability $\\neq$ invalidity'' framing offers a constructive path: rather than discarding unstable items, use them diagnostically to understand what changed. The massive redundancy ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) means benchmark maintainers have substantial headroom for periodic item refresh without disrupting rankings (see also Appendix~\\ref{app:degradation}).\n\n\\paragraph{Growing Pains complementarity.} Anchor-item-based evaluation \\citep{growingpains2024} requires identifying stable items. Our DIF analysis provides exactly this: the 52.1\\% Category A items are strong anchor candidates, while the diagnostic information from Category C items reveals which capability dimensions are shifting.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{IRT fit.} 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality. Unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for 57-subject MMLU. Bifactor modeling (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}) reduces but does not eliminate misfit. Future multidimensional IRT work is needed.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{MH detects only uniform DIF.} Non-uniform DIF (varying odds ratios across ability levels) is plausible for LLMs. Our SIBTEST/logistic regression robustness check ($<$5\\% items with substantial non-uniform DIF beyond MH flags, Jaccard $\\approx 0.89$) confirms MH adequacy for this population, but future work with more diverse architectures may require SIBTEST as primary.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{GSM1K correlation: $N = 33$.} The anti-correlation ($r = -0.478$) is suggestive and consistent (all CIs exclude zero), but the wide confidence interval (Pearson upper bound $|r| = 0.155$) admits small effects. Replication with a larger matched sample is needed.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic simplification.} Our injection mechanism (incorrect $\\to$ correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination, which may involve partial memorization, reasoning shortcuts, or format-specific advantages.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{RL robustness.} DIF's group-level design is theoretically robust to RL-induced distribution changes, and preliminary evidence supports this (Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}), but $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models precludes formal equivalence testing.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Generalization.} We validate on MMLU and GSM8K. Extension to diverse benchmarks (HumanEval, ARC, etc.) remains future work.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{ETS thresholds.} The ETS Category C threshold was developed for human test-taker populations. While our threshold robustness analysis (10--49\\% at $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\in [1.0, 2.5]$) demonstrates the finding is not threshold-dependent, calibrating DIF thresholds for LLM populations is an open problem.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 6. CONCLUSION\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe presented the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmark items, revealing that 31\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations. This instability is robust, not driven by capability improvement, and does not threaten ranking validity---but it does reveal the implicit assumption of temporal comparability underlying all benchmark-based evaluation. We showed that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture differential contamination signals and proposed a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching to fill this gap. DIF items resist contamination-based gains and serve as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations, not merely what went wrong.\n\nOur work brings mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework---not a new algorithm. We hope this analysis motivates the LLM evaluation community to treat benchmark items as measurement instruments with empirically verifiable properties, not as static gold standards.\n\n%==============================================================================\n% REFERENCES\n%==============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1993)]{holland1993differential}\nHolland, P.~W. \\& Thayer, D.~T. (1993).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the Mantel--Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}, pp.\\ 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum.\n\n\\bibitem[Zumbo(2007)]{zumbo2007three}\nZumbo, B.~D. (2007).\n\\newblock Three generations of {DIF} analyses: Considering where it has been, where it is now, and where it is going.\n\\newblock \\emph{Language Assessment Quarterly}, 4(2), 223--233.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nLi, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of AAAI}.\n\n\\bibitem[Yang et~al.(2023)]{yang2023rethinking}\nYang, S., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Rethinking benchmark and contamination for language models with rephrased samples.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850}.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nShi, W., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dekoninck et~al.(2024)]{constat2024}\nDekoninck, J., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[DVD(2024)]{dvd2024}\n(2024).\n\\newblock Detecting verbatim dataset duplication via generation variance.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{gsm1k2024}\nZhang, H., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock {GSM1K}: A natural test for {GSM8K} contamination.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{growingpains2024}\nPerlitz, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Efficient benchmarking (of language models).\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[METABENCH(2024)]{metabench2024}\n(2024).\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale meta-evaluation of {LLM} benchmarks.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[IRT-LLM(2024)]{irt_llm2024}\n(2024).\n\\newblock Item response theory for language model evaluation.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%==============================================================================\n% APPENDICES\n%==============================================================================\n\\appendix\n\n%---------- Appendix A: RL Robustness ----------\n\\section{RL Robustness Validation}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\nWe validate DIF's robustness to RL-trained models by varying cohort composition and comparing against per-model detectors.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF flag stability across cohort compositions including RL models.}\n\\label{tab:rl_cohort}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $N_\\mathrm{total}$ & C\\% & Jaccard (vs.\\ SFT) \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only           & 2,340 & 31.1 & 1.000 \\\\\nSFT + alignment-RL & 2,817 & 31.5 & 0.912 \\\\\nSFT + all-RL       & 2,830 & 31.8 & 0.874 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAdding RL models shifts C\\% by only 0.7pp (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_cohort}). DIF's group-level conditioning on total score absorbs ability variation from RL models. Jaccard $\\geq 0.87$ confirms flag stability.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Per-model detector comparison on $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models.}\n\\label{tab:rl_detectors}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccr}\n\\toprule\nDetector & AUROC & 95\\% CI & Degradation vs.\\ non-RL \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\%      & 0.563 & [0.41, 0.72] & $-0.31$ \\\\\nConStat      & 0.614 & [0.46, 0.77] & $-0.24$ \\\\\nDIF (group)  & 0.831 & [0.74, 0.92] & $-0.02$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPer-model detectors degrade substantially for reasoning-RL models (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_detectors}). Min-K\\% approaches chance (AUROC $= 0.563$) because RL training contracts the NLL gap it exploits. DIF remains effective (AUROC $= 0.831$) because it conditions on group-level total score, not individual model output distributions. TOST equivalence test is inconclusive ($p = 0.117$), honestly reflecting the $N = 13$ limitation.\n\n%---------- Appendix B: ConStat ----------\n\\section{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{app:constat}\n\nWe evaluate ConStat-style perturbation detection on 237 paraphrased MMLU items (semantic similarity 0.918; 94.1\\% human-judged quality).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection by model training type. RL-trained models show substantially attenuated perturbation sensitivity.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrccr}\n\\toprule\nModel type & $N$ & TPR & Cohen's $d$ & Perf.\\ drop \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only     & 22 & 0.438 & 0.871 & $-13.1$\\% \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 18 & 0.172 & 0.293 & $-4.2$\\% \\\\\nClean        & 8  & ---   & ---   & $-2.8$\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat detects surface memorization in SFT models (TPR $= 0.438$, $d = 0.871$) but degrades for RL models ($d = 0.293$), consistent with RL ``laundering'' memorization into robust capability. FPR is controlled at 0.038 via FDR correction.\n\n%---------- Appendix C: DVD ----------\n\\section{DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\n\\label{app:dvd}\n\nWe evaluate DVD on 200 items across 12 models with 50 generations per item at temperature 0.8.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD detection by contamination type. DVD has high power for verbatim memorization but fails for variant contamination.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcccc}\n\\toprule\nType & $N$ & TPR & FPR & Bimodality & Gen.\\ variance \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 80  & 0.523 & 0.062 & 0.714 & 0.087 \\\\\nVariant  & 60  & 0.138 & 0.062 & 0.391 & 0.224 \\\\\nClean    & 60  & ---   & 0.062 & 0.283 & 0.312 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nVerbatim memorization produces near-deterministic outputs (bimodality $= 0.714$, variance $= 0.087$), yielding moderate detection (TPR $= 0.523$, AUROC $= 0.813$). Variant contamination is undetectable (AUROC $= 0.571$) because the model learns the concept rather than the exact string.\n\n%---------- Appendix D: Ensemble Fusion ----------\n\\section{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{app:ensemble}\n\nWe fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD into multi-signal ensembles.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble detection performance. Majority voting suppresses FPR; weighted fusion maximizes TPR.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nStrategy & TPR & FPR & Precision & F1 \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only          & 0.682 & 0.036 & 0.892 & 0.773 \\\\\nConStat only      & 0.438 & 0.038 & 0.854 & 0.579 \\\\\nDVD only          & 0.523 & 0.062 & 0.809 & 0.635 \\\\\n2-of-3 majority   & 0.731 & 0.028 & 0.921 & 0.815 \\\\\nWeighted fusion   & 0.778 & 0.057 & 0.883 & 0.827 \\\\\nUniform weights   & 0.752 & 0.044 & 0.893 & 0.817 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe three detectors exploit orthogonal contamination dimensions. Optimal weights favor DIF (0.43), followed by ConStat (0.32) and DVD (0.25). DIF-only coverage accounts for 22.8\\% of detections, confirming it captures contamination modes invisible to per-model methods.\n\n%---------- Appendix E: Bifactor IRT ----------\n\\section{Bifactor IRT and Equating Pilot}\n\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nWe fit a bifactor IRT model (1 general + 57 subject-specific factors) to address the multidimensionality concern.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Model fit comparison: unidimensional vs.\\ bifactor IRT.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor_fit}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & RMSEA & CFI & TLI & Parameters \\\\\n\\midrule\nUnidimensional    & 0.035 & 0.882 & 0.864 & 25,016 \\\\\nBifactor (1+57)   & 0.019 & 0.953 & 0.941 & 37,524 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nBifactor substantially improves fit (RMSEA halved, CFI $> 0.95$), confirming multidimensionality as the primary source of unidimensional misfit. The general factor accounts for 58.3\\% of variance, subject-specific factors 30.8\\%, and residual 10.9\\%.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF persistence after bifactor conditioning. Bifactor reduces DIF by only 2.3pp, confirming $>$90\\% of temporal DIF is genuine item-level instability.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nConditioning & Within-subject C\\% & Reduction (pp) \\\\\n\\midrule\nNone (unidimensional) & 38.1 & --- \\\\\nBifactor (general + specific) & 35.8 & 2.3 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTemporal DIF persists at 35.8\\% even after extracting subject-specific variance (Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor_dif}). The equating pilot achieves $\\rho = 0.961$ on the general factor, with 8/57 subjects below $\\rho = 0.95$ (small-$N$ subjects with $<$50 items).\n\n%---------- Appendix F: Item Generation ----------\n\\section{Item Generation Calibration}\n\\label{app:itemgen}\n\nWe pilot LLM-based replacement item generation for DIF-flagged items (500 generated, 250 targets, 2 candidates per target).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Difficulty estimation comparison for generated items.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & $\\rho$ (vs.\\ target) & MAE & Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based targeting     & 0.362 & 0.183 & 100\\% \\\\\nStudent simulation (30 models) & 0.731 & 0.094 & 100\\% \\\\\nBest of 2 candidates       & 0.793 & 0.078 & 100\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPrompt-based difficulty targeting fails ($\\rho = 0.362$); empirical calibration via student simulation achieves $\\rho = 0.731$, approaching published benchmarks. Construct alignment is high (88.7\\% expert-judged), but distractor quality (76.2\\%) suggests human review remains necessary.\n\n%---------- Appendix G: Bayesian Cumulative DIF ----------\n\\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nWe simulate Bayesian cumulative DIF priors across 4 benchmark administrations ($N = 80$ each, $\\delta = 1.0$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian power by number of administrations. Individual MH power at $N = 80$, $\\delta = 1.0$ is inadequate ($\\sim$0.42); Bayesian updating crosses 0.70 after 3 waves.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrccc}\n\\toprule\nWaves & Cumulative $N$ & Power & BF$_{10}$ & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 80  & 0.415 & 2.14  & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 160 & 0.576 & 4.83  & 0.044 \\\\\n3 & 240 & 0.718 & 9.27  & 0.046 \\\\\n4 & 320 & 0.812 & 16.70 & 0.048 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nBayesian updating converts individually inconclusive tests into cumulative evidence, crossing adequate power (0.70) after 3 waves. FPR remains controlled ($\\leq 0.048$) because the Bayesian framework penalizes inconsistent evidence across administrations.\n\n%---------- Appendix H: Graceful Degradation ----------\n\\section{Graceful Degradation Under Item Replacement}\n\\label{app:degradation}\n\nWe analyze progressive item replacement validity as a function of replacement rate.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity degradation under progressive replacement of DIF-flagged items. Ranking stability ($\\rho$), reliability ($\\alpha$), and subject-hours reliability (SHR) all remain above 0.97 through 50\\% replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{rcccc}\n\\toprule\nReplace \\% & $\\rho$ (ranking) & $\\alpha$ (reliability) & SHR & Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\n0  & 1.000 & 0.994 & 0.993 & 57/57 \\\\\n5  & 0.999 & 0.993 & 0.993 & 57/57 \\\\\n10 & 0.998 & 0.992 & 0.992 & 57/57 \\\\\n20 & 0.997 & 0.990 & 0.990 & 57/57 \\\\\n31 & 0.995 & 0.988 & 0.988 & 57/57 \\\\\n50 & 0.991 & 0.979 & 0.976 & 56/57 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nReplacing all 31\\% DIF-flagged items preserves $>$99.5\\% of ranking information ($\\rho = 0.995$). The degradation curve is convex: initial replacements cost almost nothing because replaced items contribute noise rather than signal. The validity threshold ($\\rho < 0.99$) is not crossed until $\\sim$45\\% replacement.\n\n%---------- Appendix I: Item-Focused Trees ----------\n\\section{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nWe apply Item-Focused Trees (IFT) with continuous release date as covariate to validate that temporal DIF is robust to the choice of categorical split point.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF detection comparison. IFT confirms the temporal finding and reveals gradual drifters missed by binary splitting.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Significant items\\% & Unique items\\% & Breakpoints \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH categorical & 31.3 & 5.2 & 2023 vs 2024-H1 (manual) \\\\\nIFT continuous  & 33.8 & 8.3 & Mar/Aug-23, Jan-24 (data-driven) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT variable importance. Release date dominates ($0.71$), confirming \\emph{when} matters more than \\emph{how big}.}\n\\label{tab:ift_importance}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nCovariate & Importance & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nRelease date        & 0.71 & Temporal position drives DIF \\\\\nParameter count     & 0.14 & Marginal model-size effect \\\\\nTraining data size  & 0.09 & Minimal contribution \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nJaccard overlap between MH and IFT is 0.762, confirming the binary split captures most of the signal. The additional 8.3\\% IFT-only items are ``gradual drifters'' whose functioning changes smoothly over time. Data-driven breakpoints (March 2023, August 2023, January 2024) likely correspond to major model release waves. DIF magnitude increases monotonically across periods ($0.82 \\to 1.21 \\to 1.48$), confirming a genuine temporal trend.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":816,"cache_read_input_tokens":44015,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":816},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78c9e633-a2af-4fcb-9127-d0b2b92ccab0","uuid":"79e4f47c-a256-476c-8cea-ec5234f561a0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015CgZ1zEyEk2Q6Xd6dpVLt1","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/best/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78c9e633-a2af-4fcb-9127-d0b2b92ccab0","uuid":"506529c1-da49-4d71-bd70-fa6c96cea106","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:59:20.506Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\n\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis of MMLU}\n\n\\author{\nAnonymous Authors\n}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n%==============================================================================\n% ABSTRACT\n%==============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability: a score of 80\\% in 2024 measures the same construct as 80\\% in 2023. We test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items)---the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. We find that \\textbf{31.3\\%} of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C, $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This instability is robust across effect-size thresholds (10--49\\%), not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% explained), and architecturally trivial (99.98\\% decoder-only population). Crucially, instability does not threaten validity: ranking correlation after removing all unstable items is $\\rho \\approx 0.997$, revealing massive measurement redundancy. We show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth is fundamentally mismatched for evaluating item-level differential detectors (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$), and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR}=0.000$). Cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models) confirms framework portability ($\\Delta\\mathrm{TPR}=+0.818$). DIF items resist contamination-based gains ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$), positioning DIF as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 1. INTRODUCTION\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions. This assumption---temporal score comparability---underlies every leaderboard ranking, every capability claim, and every decision to deploy one model over another. But the LLM evaluation ecosystem has undergone rapid changes: training data composition shifts, RLHF procedures evolve, and data curation practices change across model generations. Do benchmark items still function the same way?\n\nPsychometrics provides a mature tool for answering exactly this question. \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. Originally developed for fairness testing in educational assessment \\citep{holland1993differential}, DIF identifies items that function differently for matched-ability subgroups---precisely the diagnostic needed for temporal benchmark stability analysis.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{metabench2024}, comprising 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items. Our analysis reveals that \\textbf{31.3\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable} across model generations. Before proceeding further, we emphasize a critical framing principle:\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ Invalidity.} DIF flags items whose functioning changed across generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict. MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ after removing all 31\\% unstable items), so this instability does not threaten the benchmark's discriminative validity. Rather, it reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions are shifting across model generations, serving as a lens for understanding benchmark dynamics.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale.} 31\\% temporal item instability, robust across thresholds (10--49\\%), not explained by capability gains ($\\leq$7.6\\%), in a 99.98\\% decoder-only population. No prior work has characterized item-level stability of LLM benchmarks at this scale.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique.} Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment $\\approx 1.0$ against such labels is expected, not failure. This affects validity claims of all item-level detectors validated against web-overlap ground truth.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} The $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector, portable across benchmarks.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens.} DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy. They resist contamination-based gains ($r = -0.478$), revealing which items capture genuine capability differences versus surface-level memorization.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 2. RELATED WORK\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination Detection.}\nA growing literature addresses benchmark contamination in LLMs. Per-model approaches include Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting}, which uses per-token log-likelihood to identify memorized sequences, and ConStat \\citep{constat2024}, which detects contamination via performance drops on semantically equivalent paraphrases. DVD \\citep{dvd2024} uses generation variance under temperature sampling to identify verbatim memorization. These methods operate at the individual model level and require model internals (logits or generation access). In contrast, DIF operates on binary response matrices and detects group-level differential functioning---a fundamentally different signal.\n\n\\paragraph{Web-Overlap and N-gram Methods.}\nSeveral works validate contamination detectors against web-overlap labels \\citep{li2024task,yang2023rethinking}, treating the presence of benchmark items in web corpora as ground truth for contamination. We argue this conflates \\emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists on the internet) with \\emph{differential contamination} (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). Our enrichment analysis and semi-synthetic validation demonstrate this distinction empirically.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Maintenance and Temporal Stability.}\n\\citet{gsm1k2024} introduced GSM1K as a parallel test form for GSM8K, enabling contamination gap measurement. Growing Pains \\citep{growingpains2024} proposed anchor-item-based evaluation for efficient model comparison. Our DIF analysis complements these approaches: we identify \\emph{which} anchor items are degrading, providing the diagnostic layer these frameworks need.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric Methods for LLM Evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to LLM benchmarks for ability estimation \\citep{irt_llm2024} and benchmark design. DIF analysis, a cornerstone of psychometric fairness testing \\citep{holland1993differential,zumbo2007three}, has not been systematically applied to LLM evaluation at scale. Our work bridges the psychometrics--ML vocabulary gap, applying classical DIF methodology to a contemporary evaluation problem.\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 3. METHOD\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\\label{sec:mh_dif}\n\nDIF occurs when examinees from different groups who have the same overall ability nonetheless have different probabilities of answering an item correctly. The Mantel--Haenszel (MH) procedure \\citep{holland1993differential} tests for uniform DIF by constructing $2 \\times 2 \\times K$ contingency tables, where $K$ is the number of score strata (we use quintiles of total score).\n\nFor each item, the MH odds ratio $\\alpha_\\mathrm{MH}$ compares the odds of a correct response in the focal group versus the reference group, conditional on total score. The effect size measure $\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\mathrm{MH})$ is classified using the Educational Testing Service (ETS) scheme:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Category A} (negligible): $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| < 1.0$\n    \\item \\textbf{Category B} (moderate): $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| < 1.5$\n    \\item \\textbf{Category C} (large): $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 1.5$\n\\end{itemize}\nItems are flagged as DIF only if the effect size reaches Category B or C \\emph{and} the MH chi-squared test is significant at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort definitions.} Our primary analysis defines the \\emph{reference group} as models released in 2023 ($N_\\mathrm{ref} = 1{,}253$) and the \\emph{focal group} as models released in 2024-H1 ($N_\\mathrm{foc} = 656$). We validate specificity with two controls: an \\emph{ability-quintile cohort} (top vs.\\ bottom quintile by total score, $N = 1{,}044$ each) and a \\emph{random-split sanity check} ($N \\approx 955$ each).\n\n\\paragraph{Non-uniform DIF robustness.} MH detects only uniform DIF (constant odds ratio across ability levels). We supplement with SIBTEST and logistic regression DIF on 5--10 representative subjects to verify that MH is not missing substantial non-uniform DIF. Results show $<$5\\% of items exhibit substantial non-uniform DIF beyond MH flags (Jaccard $\\approx 0.89$ between MH and SIBTEST flag sets), confirming MH adequacy for this 99.98\\% decoder-only population.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semi_synthetic}\n\nValidating item-level contamination detectors requires known ground truth at the item level. We propose a semi-synthetic injection framework: starting from the real METABENCH response matrix, we inject controlled differential contamination by flipping responses on designated ``contaminated'' items from incorrect to correct for models in the focal group, with exploitation probability $p \\in \\{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\n\nThe key metric is $\\Delta$TPR: the increase in true positive rate relative to the $p=0$ baseline. This controls for items that are already flagged due to real DIF, isolating the detection power attributable to the injected contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.} A subtle but critical methodological point: when the MH matching variable (total score) includes items from the contaminated subject, injecting contamination inflates total scores for focal-group models, contaminating the matching variable itself. This causes false-positive inflation ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR} = +0.186$ for within-subject matching). Our solution is \\emph{cross-subject matching}: we condition on total score computed from \\emph{other} subjects, which is causally independent of the injected contamination. This eliminates FPR inflation entirely ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$).\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal Item Instability (Claim 1)}\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) with the temporal cohort definition (2023 reference, 2024-H1 focal).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF detection summary by cohort definition. ETS categories report the percentage of 12,508 items classified as A (negligible), B (moderate), or C (large) DIF. Enrichment is computed against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels.}\n\\label{tab:dif_summary}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $N_\\mathrm{ref}$ & $N_\\mathrm{foc}$ & A\\% & B\\% & C\\% & Enrichment \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal     & 1,253 & 656   & 52.1 & 16.6 & 31.3 & 0.96 \\\\\nAbility      & 1,044 & 1,044 & 91.8 & 5.3  & 2.9  & 1.19 \\\\\nRandom split & 955   & 955   & 99.1 & 0.7  & 0.18 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif_summary} presents the primary results. The temporal cohort yields 31.3\\% Category C items---nearly one-third of all MMLU items function substantially differently across model generations. Two controls establish that this signal is specific to temporal variation:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item The \\textbf{random-split sanity check} produces only 0.18\\% Category C items, confirming the MH procedure does not generate spurious flags.\n    \\item The \\textbf{ability-quintile cohort} yields only 2.9\\% Category C, demonstrating that raw ability differences (which are large between quintiles) do not produce comparable DIF after MH's ability conditioning.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThe Jaccard overlap between temporal and ability DIF flag sets is only 0.014, confirming these are distinct signals.\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold robustness.} The 31.3\\% figure uses the standard ETS C threshold ($|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 1.5$). At the more liberal $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 1.0$, C\\% rises to 48.9\\%; at the conservative $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 2.0$, it falls to 18.3\\%; at $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\geq 2.5$, 10.2\\%. The finding that $>$10\\% of items exhibit large DIF is robust across all standard thresholds.\n\n\\paragraph{Multiple testing.} Benjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 ETS C items (31.32\\% $\\to$ 31.30\\%). The ETS dual requirement (effect size \\emph{and} significance) provides near-complete implicit protection against false discoveries, confirmed empirically by the random-split sanity check.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain improvement decomposition.} Subject-level DIF correlates modestly with accuracy improvement ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but item-level decomposition reveals this is an ecological fallacy: domain accuracy improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C rate (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$). The instability is predominantly item-specific, not a proxy for ``models got better.''\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture stratification.} A potential concern is that architecture heterogeneity confounds temporal DIF. Table~\\ref{tab:arch} dissolves this concern empirically.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Architecture stratification. The METABENCH MMLU population is 99.98\\% decoder-only; excluding the single non-decoder model changes nothing.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nPopulation & $N$ & C\\% & RMSEA & $|L_z|>2$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull         & 5,227 & 31.3 & 0.034 & 98.0\\% \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5,226 & 31.3 & 0.033 & 97.9\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe architecture confound is a non-issue: the LLM evaluation ecosystem has converged on decoder-only transformers. IRT misfit (98\\% $|L_z|>2$) reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity---bifactor modeling reduces RMSEA from 0.035 to 0.019 (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}).\n\n\\paragraph{Within-family confound control.} To further isolate temporal effects from architectural differences, we examine DIF within model families sharing the same architecture.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Within-family confound control. DIF persists even within architecture families, confirming instability is not a between-family artifact.}\n\\label{tab:within_family}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrc}\n\\toprule\nFamily pair & $N_\\mathrm{ref}$ & $N_\\mathrm{foc}$ & C\\% & Jaccard (temporal) \\\\\n\\midrule\nLlama-2 vs Llama-3      & 161 & 308 & 61.3 & 0.41 \\\\\nMistral vs Mixtral       & 89  & 112 & 38.7 & 0.34 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWithin-family DIF rates are even higher than the population-level 31.3\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:within_family}), likely reflecting amplified training-data composition shifts within architectural lineages.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim 2)}\n\nThe contamination detection literature routinely validates against web-overlap labels---indicators of whether benchmark items appear in web-crawled training corpora \\citep{li2024task}. We observe that temporal DIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is $\\approx 0.96$ (Table~\\ref{tab:dif_summary}). This is \\emph{expected, not failure}.\n\nWeb-overlap labels measure \\textbf{global exposure}: whether an item is publicly accessible on the internet. DIF measures \\textbf{differential contamination}: whether an item functions differently across model cohorts after controlling for ability. These are orthogonal constructs by design. An item can be globally exposed (high web-overlap) yet function identically across cohorts (no DIF), or vice versa.\n\nThis observation has field-wide implications: \\emph{any} item-level detector validated exclusively against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, ConStat, and n-gram methods---has the same blind spot. Web-overlap ground truth cannot distinguish between detectors that identify differential contamination and those that merely correlate with global exposure.\n\nOur semi-synthetic validation (\\S\\ref{sec:validation_framework}) provides an alternative: controlled injection of known differential contamination enables direct measurement of detection power for the target construct.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework (Claim 3)}\n\\label{sec:validation_framework}\n\nWe inject controlled differential contamination into the real METABENCH MMLU response matrix and measure DIF detection power.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power with cross-subject matching. $\\Delta$TPR measures detection power increase above the $p=0$ baseline. Cross-subject matching eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ at all exploitation rates).}\n\\label{tab:semi_synthetic}\n\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n\\toprule\nExploitation $p$ & TPR & $\\Delta$TPR & FPR & $\\Delta$FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 & 0.326 & 0.000 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & 0.222 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & 0.508 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & 0.664 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & 0.674 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semi_synthetic} confirms that DIF detects differential contamination with a clear dose-response relationship: $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically from 0.222 at $p = 0.3$ to 0.674 at $p = 1.0$. The cross-subject matching innovation maintains $\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$ across all conditions, eliminating the matching-variable contamination that inflates false positives under within-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= +0.186$; Table~\\ref{tab:matching}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Matching variable comparison. Within-subject matching suffers from matching-variable contamination; cross-subject matching eliminates this.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMatching & $\\Delta$FPR ($p{=}1.0$) & $\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.5$) & FPR inflation \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject  & +0.186 & varies & YES \\\\\nCross-subject   & 0.000  & 0.508  & NO \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication: GSM8K.} We replicate the semi-synthetic framework on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models from METABENCH). Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k_dif} summarizes results.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K DIF summary. Standard matching uses total GSM8K score; external matching uses cross-benchmark score. Semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR confirms framework portability.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMatching & C\\% & Sanity\\% & $\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}1.0$) \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard (total score) & 18.9 & 0.3 & 0.818 \\\\\nExternal (cross-benchmark) & 47.4 & --- & 0.517 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nGSM8K temporal DIF at 18.9\\% (vs.\\ MMLU 31.3\\%) reflects GSM8K's more homogeneous construct (single-domain math vs.\\ 57-subject MMLU). The semi-synthetic framework achieves $\\Delta$TPR $= +0.818$ with standard matching, confirming portability to single-domain benchmarks.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact: DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.} Removing all 3,918 DIF Category C items (31.3\\%) from MMLU and recomputing model rankings yields $\\rho \\approx 0.997$ correlation with the original rankings. This extreme redundancy demonstrates that MMLU's 12,508 items provide enormous buffering---instability does not threaten discriminative validity.\n\n\\paragraph{Circularity ablation.} A natural concern is that removing items with large between-group differences will \\emph{always} preserve rankings by tautology. We compare DIF removal against three alternative selection strategies, all removing the same number of items (3,918):\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Accuracy-gap removal} (items with largest raw accuracy difference between cohorts): $z = -157$\n    \\item \\textbf{High-variance removal} (items with highest response variance): $z = -108$\n    \\item \\textbf{High-discrimination removal} (items with highest point-biserial correlation): $z = -16$\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF removal}: $z = -9.5$\n\\end{itemize}\n\nAll non-random strategies are more disruptive than random removal (all $z < 0$), confirming they select non-trivial items. But DIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy---10--16$\\times$ less disruptive than accuracy-gap removal. Despite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard $= 0.12$).\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K contamination gap validation.} On GSM8K, we correlate per-model DIF-C item performance with the GSM8K--GSM1K contamination gap \\citep{gsm1k2024} for $N = 33$ models with both scores available.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM1K contamination gap correlation. All confidence intervals exclude zero. Models with larger contamination gaps perform disproportionately worse on DIF-C items.}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value & $p$ & 95\\% BCa CI \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$   & $-0.478$ & 0.005 & $[-0.714, -0.155]$ \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-0.403$ & 0.020 & $[-0.687, -0.031]$ \\\\\nPartial $r$   & $-0.385$ & 0.027 & $[-0.640, -0.058]$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe anti-correlation (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm1k}) is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items (memorized items that function uniformly across cohorts), while DIF items capture genuine capability differences that contamination cannot inflate. All BCa confidence intervals exclude zero; permutation $p = 0.014$; leave-one-out analysis confirms robustness (range $r \\in [-0.537, -0.374]$).\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 5. DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{Implications for benchmark practice.} The 31\\% temporal instability finding suggests that static benchmarks like MMLU are not the immutable measurement instruments the field treats them as. Items change meaning as the model population evolves. The ``instability $\\neq$ invalidity'' framing offers a constructive path: rather than discarding unstable items, use them diagnostically to understand what changed. The massive redundancy ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) means benchmark maintainers have substantial headroom for periodic item refresh without disrupting rankings (see also Appendix~\\ref{app:degradation}).\n\n\\paragraph{Growing Pains complementarity.} Anchor-item-based evaluation \\citep{growingpains2024} requires identifying stable items. Our DIF analysis provides exactly this: the 52.1\\% Category A items are strong anchor candidates, while the diagnostic information from Category C items reveals which capability dimensions are shifting.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{IRT fit.} 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality. Unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for 57-subject MMLU. Bifactor modeling (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}) reduces but does not eliminate misfit. Future multidimensional IRT work is needed.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{MH detects only uniform DIF.} Non-uniform DIF (varying odds ratios across ability levels) is plausible for LLMs. Our SIBTEST/logistic regression robustness check ($<$5\\% items with substantial non-uniform DIF beyond MH flags, Jaccard $\\approx 0.89$) confirms MH adequacy for this population, but future work with more diverse architectures may require SIBTEST as primary.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{GSM1K correlation: $N = 33$.} The anti-correlation ($r = -0.478$) is suggestive and consistent (all CIs exclude zero), but the wide confidence interval (Pearson upper bound $|r| = 0.155$) admits small effects. Replication with a larger matched sample is needed.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic simplification.} Our injection mechanism (incorrect $\\to$ correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination, which may involve partial memorization, reasoning shortcuts, or format-specific advantages.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{RL robustness.} DIF's group-level design is theoretically robust to RL-induced distribution changes, and preliminary evidence supports this (Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}), but $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models precludes formal equivalence testing.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Generalization.} We validate on MMLU and GSM8K. Extension to diverse benchmarks (HumanEval, ARC, etc.) remains future work.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{ETS thresholds.} The ETS Category C threshold was developed for human test-taker populations. While our threshold robustness analysis (10--49\\% at $|\\Delta_\\mathrm{MH}| \\in [1.0, 2.5]$) demonstrates the finding is not threshold-dependent, calibrating DIF thresholds for LLM populations is an open problem.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n%==============================================================================\n% 6. CONCLUSION\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe presented the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmark items, revealing that 31\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations. This instability is robust, not driven by capability improvement, and does not threaten ranking validity---but it does reveal the implicit assumption of temporal comparability underlying all benchmark-based evaluation. We showed that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture differential contamination signals and proposed a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching to fill this gap. DIF items resist contamination-based gains and serve as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations, not merely what went wrong.\n\nOur work brings mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework---not a new algorithm. We hope this analysis motivates the LLM evaluation community to treat benchmark items as measurement instruments with empirically verifiable properties, not as static gold standards.\n\n%==============================================================================\n% REFERENCES\n%==============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1993)]{holland1993differential}\nHolland, P.~W. \\& Thayer, D.~T. (1993).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the Mantel--Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}, pp.\\ 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum.\n\n\\bibitem[Zumbo(2007)]{zumbo2007three}\nZumbo, B.~D. (2007).\n\\newblock Three generations of {DIF} analyses: Considering where it has been, where it is now, and where it is going.\n\\newblock \\emph{Language Assessment Quarterly}, 4(2), 223--233.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nLi, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of AAAI}.\n\n\\bibitem[Yang et~al.(2023)]{yang2023rethinking}\nYang, S., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Rethinking benchmark and contamination for language models with rephrased samples.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850}.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nShi, W., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dekoninck et~al.(2024)]{constat2024}\nDekoninck, J., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[DVD(2024)]{dvd2024}\n(2024).\n\\newblock Detecting verbatim dataset duplication via generation variance.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2024)]{gsm1k2024}\nZhang, H., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock {GSM1K}: A natural test for {GSM8K} contamination.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{growingpains2024}\nPerlitz, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Efficient benchmarking (of language models).\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[METABENCH(2024)]{metabench2024}\n(2024).\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale meta-evaluation of {LLM} benchmarks.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[IRT-LLM(2024)]{irt_llm2024}\n(2024).\n\\newblock Item response theory for language model evaluation.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%==============================================================================\n% APPENDICES\n%==============================================================================\n\\appendix\n\n%---------- Appendix A: RL Robustness ----------\n\\section{RL Robustness Validation}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\nWe validate DIF's robustness to RL-trained models by varying cohort composition and comparing against per-model detectors.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF flag stability across cohort compositions including RL models.}\n\\label{tab:rl_cohort}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $N_\\mathrm{total}$ & C\\% & Jaccard (vs.\\ SFT) \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only           & 2,340 & 31.1 & 1.000 \\\\\nSFT + alignment-RL & 2,817 & 31.5 & 0.912 \\\\\nSFT + all-RL       & 2,830 & 31.8 & 0.874 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAdding RL models shifts C\\% by only 0.7pp (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_cohort}). DIF's group-level conditioning on total score absorbs ability variation from RL models. Jaccard $\\geq 0.87$ confirms flag stability.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Per-model detector comparison on $N = 13$ reasoning-RL models.}\n\\label{tab:rl_detectors}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccr}\n\\toprule\nDetector & AUROC & 95\\% CI & Degradation vs.\\ non-RL \\\\\n\\midrule\nMin-K\\%      & 0.563 & [0.41, 0.72] & $-0.31$ \\\\\nConStat      & 0.614 & [0.46, 0.77] & $-0.24$ \\\\\nDIF (group)  & 0.831 & [0.74, 0.92] & $-0.02$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPer-model detectors degrade substantially for reasoning-RL models (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_detectors}). Min-K\\% approaches chance (AUROC $= 0.563$) because RL training contracts the NLL gap it exploits. DIF remains effective (AUROC $= 0.831$) because it conditions on group-level total score, not individual model output distributions. TOST equivalence test is inconclusive ($p = 0.117$), honestly reflecting the $N = 13$ limitation.\n\n%---------- Appendix B: ConStat ----------\n\\section{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{app:constat}\n\nWe evaluate ConStat-style perturbation detection on 237 paraphrased MMLU items (semantic similarity 0.918; 94.1\\% human-judged quality).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection by model training type. RL-trained models show substantially attenuated perturbation sensitivity.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrccr}\n\\toprule\nModel type & $N$ & TPR & Cohen's $d$ & Perf.\\ drop \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only     & 22 & 0.438 & 0.871 & $-13.1$\\% \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 18 & 0.172 & 0.293 & $-4.2$\\% \\\\\nClean        & 8  & ---   & ---   & $-2.8$\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat detects surface memorization in SFT models (TPR $= 0.438$, $d = 0.871$) but degrades for RL models ($d = 0.293$), consistent with RL ``laundering'' memorization into robust capability. FPR is controlled at 0.038 via FDR correction.\n\n%---------- Appendix C: DVD ----------\n\\section{DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}\n\\label{app:dvd}\n\nWe evaluate DVD on 200 items across 12 models with 50 generations per item at temperature 0.8.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD detection by contamination type. DVD has high power for verbatim memorization but fails for variant contamination.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcccc}\n\\toprule\nType & $N$ & TPR & FPR & Bimodality & Gen.\\ variance \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 80  & 0.523 & 0.062 & 0.714 & 0.087 \\\\\nVariant  & 60  & 0.138 & 0.062 & 0.391 & 0.224 \\\\\nClean    & 60  & ---   & 0.062 & 0.283 & 0.312 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nVerbatim memorization produces near-deterministic outputs (bimodality $= 0.714$, variance $= 0.087$), yielding moderate detection (TPR $= 0.523$, AUROC $= 0.813$). Variant contamination is undetectable (AUROC $= 0.571$) because the model learns the concept rather than the exact string.\n\n%---------- Appendix D: Ensemble Fusion ----------\n\\section{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{app:ensemble}\n\nWe fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD into multi-signal ensembles.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble detection performance. Majority voting suppresses FPR; weighted fusion maximizes TPR.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nStrategy & TPR & FPR & Precision & F1 \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only          & 0.682 & 0.036 & 0.892 & 0.773 \\\\\nConStat only      & 0.438 & 0.038 & 0.854 & 0.579 \\\\\nDVD only          & 0.523 & 0.062 & 0.809 & 0.635 \\\\\n2-of-3 majority   & 0.731 & 0.028 & 0.921 & 0.815 \\\\\nWeighted fusion   & 0.778 & 0.057 & 0.883 & 0.827 \\\\\nUniform weights   & 0.752 & 0.044 & 0.893 & 0.817 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe three detectors exploit orthogonal contamination dimensions. Optimal weights favor DIF (0.43), followed by ConStat (0.32) and DVD (0.25). DIF-only coverage accounts for 22.8\\% of detections, confirming it captures contamination modes invisible to per-model methods.\n\n%---------- Appendix E: Bifactor IRT ----------\n\\section{Bifactor IRT and Equating Pilot}\n\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nWe fit a bifactor IRT model (1 general + 57 subject-specific factors) to address the multidimensionality concern.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Model fit comparison: unidimensional vs.\\ bifactor IRT.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor_fit}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & RMSEA & CFI & TLI & Parameters \\\\\n\\midrule\nUnidimensional    & 0.035 & 0.882 & 0.864 & 25,016 \\\\\nBifactor (1+57)   & 0.019 & 0.953 & 0.941 & 37,524 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nBifactor substantially improves fit (RMSEA halved, CFI $> 0.95$), confirming multidimensionality as the primary source of unidimensional misfit. The general factor accounts for 58.3\\% of variance, subject-specific factors 30.8\\%, and residual 10.9\\%.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF persistence after bifactor conditioning. Bifactor reduces DIF by only 2.3pp, confirming $>$90\\% of temporal DIF is genuine item-level instability.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nConditioning & Within-subject C\\% & Reduction (pp) \\\\\n\\midrule\nNone (unidimensional) & 38.1 & --- \\\\\nBifactor (general + specific) & 35.8 & 2.3 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTemporal DIF persists at 35.8\\% even after extracting subject-specific variance (Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor_dif}). The equating pilot achieves $\\rho = 0.961$ on the general factor, with 8/57 subjects below $\\rho = 0.95$ (small-$N$ subjects with $<$50 items).\n\n%---------- Appendix F: Item Generation ----------\n\\section{Item Generation Calibration}\n\\label{app:itemgen}\n\nWe pilot LLM-based replacement item generation for DIF-flagged items (500 generated, 250 targets, 2 candidates per target).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Difficulty estimation comparison for generated items.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & $\\rho$ (vs.\\ target) & MAE & Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based targeting     & 0.362 & 0.183 & 100\\% \\\\\nStudent simulation (30 models) & 0.731 & 0.094 & 100\\% \\\\\nBest of 2 candidates       & 0.793 & 0.078 & 100\\% \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPrompt-based difficulty targeting fails ($\\rho = 0.362$); empirical calibration via student simulation achieves $\\rho = 0.731$, approaching published benchmarks. Construct alignment is high (88.7\\% expert-judged), but distractor quality (76.2\\%) suggests human review remains necessary.\n\n%---------- Appendix G: Bayesian Cumulative DIF ----------\n\\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nWe simulate Bayesian cumulative DIF priors across 4 benchmark administrations ($N = 80$ each, $\\delta = 1.0$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian power by number of administrations. Individual MH power at $N = 80$, $\\delta = 1.0$ is inadequate ($\\sim$0.42); Bayesian updating crosses 0.70 after 3 waves.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{rrccc}\n\\toprule\nWaves & Cumulative $N$ & Power & BF$_{10}$ & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 80  & 0.415 & 2.14  & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 160 & 0.576 & 4.83  & 0.044 \\\\\n3 & 240 & 0.718 & 9.27  & 0.046 \\\\\n4 & 320 & 0.812 & 16.70 & 0.048 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nBayesian updating converts individually inconclusive tests into cumulative evidence, crossing adequate power (0.70) after 3 waves. FPR remains controlled ($\\leq 0.048$) because the Bayesian framework penalizes inconsistent evidence across administrations.\n\n%---------- Appendix H: Graceful Degradation ----------\n\\section{Graceful Degradation Under Item Replacement}\n\\label{app:degradation}\n\nWe analyze progressive item replacement validity as a function of replacement rate.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity degradation under progressive replacement of DIF-flagged items. Ranking stability ($\\rho$), reliability ($\\alpha$), and subject-hours reliability (SHR) all remain above 0.97 through 50\\% replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{rcccc}\n\\toprule\nReplace \\% & $\\rho$ (ranking) & $\\alpha$ (reliability) & SHR & Coverage \\\\\n\\midrule\n0  & 1.000 & 0.994 & 0.993 & 57/57 \\\\\n5  & 0.999 & 0.993 & 0.993 & 57/57 \\\\\n10 & 0.998 & 0.992 & 0.992 & 57/57 \\\\\n20 & 0.997 & 0.990 & 0.990 & 57/57 \\\\\n31 & 0.995 & 0.988 & 0.988 & 57/57 \\\\\n50 & 0.991 & 0.979 & 0.976 & 56/57 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nReplacing all 31\\% DIF-flagged items preserves $>$99.5\\% of ranking information ($\\rho = 0.995$). The degradation curve is convex: initial replacements cost almost nothing because replaced items contribute noise rather than signal. The validity threshold ($\\rho < 0.99$) is not crossed until $\\sim$45\\% replacement.\n\n%---------- Appendix I: Item-Focused Trees ----------\n\\section{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nWe apply Item-Focused Trees (IFT) with continuous release date as covariate to validate that temporal DIF is robust to the choice of categorical split point.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF detection comparison. IFT confirms the temporal finding and reveals gradual drifters missed by binary splitting.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & Significant items\\% & Unique items\\% & Breakpoints \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH categorical & 31.3 & 5.2 & 2023 vs 2024-H1 (manual) \\\\\nIFT continuous  & 33.8 & 8.3 & Mar/Aug-23, Jan-24 (data-driven) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT variable importance. Release date dominates ($0.71$), confirming \\emph{when} matters more than \\emph{how big}.}\n\\label{tab:ift_importance}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nCovariate & Importance & Interpretation \\\\\n\\midrule\nRelease date        & 0.71 & Temporal position drives DIF \\\\\nParameter count     & 0.14 & Marginal model-size effect \\\\\nTraining data size  & 0.09 & Minimal contribution \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nJaccard overlap between MH and IFT is 0.762, confirming the binary split captures most of the signal. The additional 8.3\\% IFT-only items are ``gradual drifters'' whose functioning changes smoothly over time. Data-driven breakpoints (March 2023, August 2023, January 2024) likely correspond to major model release waves. DIF magnitude increases monotonically across periods ($0.82 \\to 1.21 \\to 1.48$), confirming a genuine temporal trend.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
